{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check; git status --short; sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; date -u\"", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py\nImplemented the two-entry fall-through fetch queue in cores/bench only.\n\nif_stage.sv owns the independent fetch PC and two explicit 97-bit flop\nbundles containing PC, original instruction, predicted direction and the\nsaved next fetch address. Empty ready transactions transfer directly into\nthe original ID/EX boundary without a queue write. Backend holds permit\nindependent fetch acceptance until both entries are occupied. A full FIFO\ncan consume its head and refill its tail on the same edge. Buffered heads\nremain valid when instruction memory is unavailable. Reset and accepted\nEX recovery cancel every push/pop, discard buffered wrong-path entries,\nand restore PC=0 or the full architectural redirect address respectively.\n\ncore.sv connects actual ID/EX acceptance to FIFO consumption. Decode,\nencoded source tags, immediate generation, register reads and youngest\nEX > MEM > WB/RF forwarding all operate on the selected raw head. Operand\nvalues are captured only at the original ID/EX edge. hazard_unit.sv keeps\nbackend holds and the one-cycle load interlock independent of fetch\ncapacity, and checks source dependencies only for an available head and\na valid EX load. The predictor still looks up the independent fetch PC;\nnonblocking FIFO capture retains its old-row metadata on training edges.\nThe exact 64-row policy, predictor range/alignment guards, execution\nunits, immediate EX recovery, retirement boundary, nret=1 and external\nports are unchanged.\n\nImplementation details that differ from the advisory plan:\n\n- Occupancy uses exactly two flop bits encoded EMPTY=00, ONE=01,\n  FULL=11. Direct bit equations avoid an additional empty-state decode\n  on the instruction mux. The queue uses named head/tail registers,\n  rather than an indexed array, so there is no FIFO RAM inference.\n- Empty-bus unavailabil\n…[2056 of 5128 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1c110b015d4f68a6]…\n\nso the scored median is 75.478 MHz. Option 0 uses 4,913 logic cells,\n2,051 registers, 32 LUT-RAMs, 5 BSRAMs and 2 DSPs. The critical path runs\nfrom FIFO occupancy through selected-head register reads into the\noriginal ID/EX operands. Reports are under cores/bench/generated/timing/.\n\nJoint fitness is 75.478e6 * 10 / 3,978,917 = 189.695 iterations/second.\nThe champion's 81.537 MHz and 4,197,447 bracket cycles yield 194.254\niterations/second. The prototype reduces bracket cycles by 5.21%, but\njoint fitness regresses by 2.35%. At these cycles the 4.6% improvement\nthreshold would require approximately 80.847 MHz median Fmax.\n\nConcern: FIFO selection and its control fanout cost more timing than the\ncycle reduction recovers. Small control simplifications improved the\ninitial prototype's 69.793 MHz median to 75.478 MHz, but the final measured\nresult still does not meet the acceptance threshold. This is a working,\ncorrectness-checked isolated prototype, not a claimed fitness improvement.\nMon Oct  5 08:21:26 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
