{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git status --short; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Implementation\n\nAdded a full 32-bit effective address to ID/EX. It captures finalized rs1\nplus the decoded immediate on the same accepted ID edge as both raw source\noperands, with modulo-2^32 arithmetic and the existing flush/reset priority.\nThe external data address is driven exclusively by this registered word.\n\nMoved load extraction, store replication/masks, memory alignment traps and\nthe data-bus handshake into EX. Only valid, legal, aligned requests outside\nreset wait for io_dmemReady. An accepted load is the youngest EX producer\nand forwards directly to a younger instruction's ID acceptance edge.\n\nRemoved the load-use bubble and downstream memory freeze. A waiting EX\nrequest holds ID/EX and fetch while EX/MEM becomes a completely cleared\nbubble and older MEM/WB instructions drain once. MEM now transfers only\nsaved results and metadata. Raw read data, replicated write data, aligned\nRVFI address and both masks are captured on EX acceptance. Recovery and\npredictor updates use the same acceptance protocol. Divider launch,\nindependent iteration, sticky completion and once-only consume remain.\n\nForwarding has an explicit unavailable-youngest-writer selection during\nan EX wait, so an older same-rd MEM/WB writer is not exposed as its\nreplacement. Decode is held for the entire wait. Raw source values and\nunused encoded source fields retain the existing RVFI convention.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed topology. There is no remaining\ndownstream backpressure source after removing live MEM transactions, so\nthe obsolete EX/MEM freeze and MEM/WB hold ports were removed rather than\nretained as tied-off inputs. The existing native multiplier, iterative\ndivider, predictor, decoder legality rules, nret=1 and top-level port set\nwere\n…[2294 of 5366 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:40c1ab8d1aa1caa1]…\nmpion.\nGowin reported 4,619 logic cells, 1,787 registers, 32 LUT-RAMs, five BSRAMs\nand two DSPs. The worst option-0 path ran from ID/EX rs2 through EX control,\npredictor/recovery and ID to a dmem BSRAM endpoint (21.332 ns arrival,\n-11.038 ns slack at the evaluation constraint). The initial option-0\nreport also directly exposed EX ALU/producer forwarding through the ID\neffective-address adder to a dmem endpoint (22.687 ns arrival).\n\nA further equivalent request qualifier using only decoded memory legality\nand memory alignment passed lint, formal, all unit tests and five-ELF\ncosim, but measured only 151.40 iterations/second (median 60.461 MHz).\nIt was reverted; the higher-scoring effective-trap qualifier is retained.\n\nThe acceptance threshold is strictly greater than 203.1855 iterations/sec.\nThe hypothesis FAILED the fitness acceptance criterion. The principal remaining\nconcern is the added address adder after the EX producer/ID forwarding\nlogic, including load-response forwarding into the next memory address.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
