{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n?? cores/bench/test/test_branch_predictor.py\n# Small bimodal predictor with direct fetch target decoding\n\nImplemented the proposed 128-entry, two-bit saturating direction table,\nindexed by PC[8:2] and reset to weakly not taken. Fetch decodes legal branch\nfunct3 values and JAL, forms signed B/J immediates, and predicts only aligned\ndirect targets. Other instructions and misaligned targets use PC+4.\n\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact predicted next PC. Accepted fetches advance\nto that prediction; existing interlocks and bus backpressure hold the PC.\nEX retains the existing forwarded operands, comparisons, target and link\ncalculations, and trap behavior. Its resolved next PC drives both recovery\nand RVFI pc_wdata. Recovery occurs only on a valid advancing instruction's\nprediction mismatch. Legal conditional branches train once on EX acceptance;\nreset, bubbles, dmem holds, illegal encodings, and branch misalignment traps\ncannot train. An older dmem stall defers both recovery and training until\nrelease, when recovery overrides imem backpressure.\n\nThe only implementation deviations are separating the dmem hold equations\nfrom redirect-dependent hazard logic to avoid a combinational scheduling\ncycle, adding a guarded core_pkg include for the requested glob-order lint\ncommand, and adding a dedicated predictor unit-test file. The pipeline test\nbus model now samples transfers at their acceptance edge and applies stores\nonly with dmemReady asserted. No architectural decoder, arithmetic,\nretirement-width, memory-port, or RVFI-port changes were needed.\n\nDirected regressions cover learned backward loops and exits; all legal\nforward branch comparisons; JAL and JALR; reserved branch encodings;\nmisaligned taken transfers; counter aliases; saturating-counter hysteresis;\nreset v\n…[1334 of 4406 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c21fac03d242e9d5]…\nnch: pass, all three placements completed and\n  the canonical CoreMark correctness gate passed. Fmax values were\n  5.191 / 5.408 / 5.408 MHz, giving a median of 5.408 MHz. Ten iterations\n  took 4,202,272 marker-bracketed cycles, yielding 12.87 iterations/second.\n  Area was 13,344 logic cells and 748 registers, with 8 LUT-RAM, 6 BSRAM,\n  and 6 DSP.\n\nRemaining concerns are counter aliasing and the extra table/metadata area\naffecting other workloads and Gowin placements. Against the recorded\nbaseline (4,491,485 cycles, 5.442 MHz median, 12.12 iterations/second),\niterations/cycle improved by 6.88%, median Fmax decreased by 0.62%, and\nreported fitness improved by 6.19%, exceeding the 4.6% threshold. The\nmeasured IPC improvement was smaller than the proposed nine-percent\nestimate. Logic increased by 1,032 cells and registers by 288. The\norchestrator still performs its own acceptance gates after this iteration.\nArithmetic was not changed, so formal-deep was not run; the existing\nreal-arithmetic cocotb suite passed.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
