{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; git diff --stat; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv       |   1 +\n cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv       |   7 +-\n cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv       |   3 +\n cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | 187 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n 4 files changed, 195 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n# Implementation notes\n\nImplemented the full direct-target registration hypothesis without RTL deviations.\n`id_ex_t` has exactly one additional 32-bit `pc_relative_target` word. ID captures\nthe modular `in.pc + imm` sum on its existing acceptance edge; the existing whole\nbundle reset, flush, bubble and hold behavior applies to it. EX uses the saved\nword for branch/JAL destinations, prediction verification, training (including\nnot-taken branches) and direct recovery. JALR still adds finalized rs1 and the\nimmediate in EX and clears bit zero. PC+4, links, predictor policy/state,\nacceptance/recovery timing, arithmetic, traps, RVFI and nret=1 are unchanged.\n\nAll 34 existing pipeline cases remain. Three directed cases additionally cover\npositive/negative direct immediates, 16-bit carries/borrows, signed-address\nboundaries, full-width positive/negative wraparound, aligned/misaligned transfers,\nwrapped JAL links, not-taken training targets, reset of a held direct JAL, every\ninterlock, wrong-path effects and stale JAL predictions whose new targets differ\nonly above bit 19. The checked bus harness verifies accepted PC/instruction/imm/\ntarget pairing on every edge, whole-bundle stability during holds, and zeroing on\nreset/flush/bubbles. Sparse high-address cocotb programs deliberately exercise\narchitectural target width; they are not fitness workloads.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required strict Verilator lint: pass, no warnings.\n- Strict Verilator cosim build (`-Wall`, without `-Wno-fatal`): pass.\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  **pass, 53 passed / 0 failed, first attempt; no RTL fixes or counterexamples.**\n- All seven unit-test runners passed: 37 pipeline cases, 3 predictor cases,\n  30 decoder cases, 8 immediate cases, 7 regfile cases, and 32 arithmetic cases\n  in each of hardware and ALTOPS modes (149 total cocotb cases).\n- Selftest and random1/random2/random3: all eight full retirement comparisons\n  passed, each program in zero-wait and random imem/dmem stall modes.\n- Canonical stalled CoreMark: CRC/completion/bounds validation passed; both\n  timing markers validated; exactly **4,197,447** bracketed cycles for 10\n  iterations, or 10/4197447 iterations/cycle.\n\nFresh vendor scoring (`python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options`) passed\nall placements. Gowin options 0/1/2 measured **79.190 / 83.917 / 83.917 MHz**;\nthe actual median is **83.917 MHz**. Area is 4,618 logic cells, 1,889 registers,\n32 LUT-RAM units, 5 BSRAMs and 2 DSPs. A fresh canonical CoreMark run using the\nstrictly built cosim binary again validated CRCs, completion, bounds and markers\nat exactly 4,197,447 cycles. Fitness is therefore\n`83.917e6 * 10 / 4197447 = 199.923906` iterations/second: **2.92093%** above\n194.25 (rounded integer improvement 3%, medium confidence).\n\nThis is below the required **203.186** iterations/second acceptance score;\n**no champion win is claimed**. The residual worst paths traverse EX ALU and\noperand forwarding into ID capture, rather than the registered direct target.\nAt unchanged cycles, acceptance still requires approximately 85.29 MHz median.\nThe fresh local results reproduce the measured prototype. Vendor reports are\nunder `cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p{0,1,2}/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
