{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd={'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r10s0','title':'Prepare five-bit shift control before execute','category':'micro_opt','motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 bracketed cycles. A fresh unchanged Gowin build reproduces 81.537 MHz; its worst path launches from registered alu_src through EX operand selection, ALU logic and ID forwarding into multiplier operand capture. Preparing multiplier operands before EX previously improved fitness, but registering complete ordinary ALU operands adds substantial datapath cost. No LESSONS.md exists. History contains no dedicated shift-amount preparation attempt.''','hypothesis':'''Prepare only the five-bit shift amount at the existing ID/EX acceptance edge and launch hardware shifters directly from raw finalized rs1 and that registered amount. This is one local shifter-input micro-optimization, with no stage, latency, scheduling, predictor, arithmetic-algorithm or bus-handshake change.\n\nAdd shift_amount[4:0] to id_ex_t. In ID's existing normal capture arm assign it from dec_alu_src ? imm[4:0] : rs2_final[4:0], after youngest-producer forwarding. Capture on exactly the same edge as finalized operands and controls; preserve whole-bundle clearing on reset/flush/bubbles and exact holds during dmem or division waits. Do not precompute a full 32-bit operand or add a new data register bank.\n\nAdd a default-disabled PREPARED_SHIFT parameter plus shift_a and shift_amount inputs to alu. EX enables that mode, connecting shift_a directly to in.rs1_val and shift_amount to the new five flops. For ALU_SLL/SRL/SRA use those prepared inputs. Leave the three native shifter expressions and existing result case statement intact. The validated decoder guarantees these operations do not select AUIPC's PC operand, so raw finalized rs1 is exactly their architectural left operand. The captured amount is the low five bits of the exact original ALU B operand for both register and immediate shifts. Preserve arithmetic right shift sign extension and RV32 modulo-32 shift counts.\n\nStandalone ALU users keep their original a/b behavior with the parameter disabled. Every other operation keeps original alu_a/alu_b operand routing and operation selection. Preserve direct native multiplier launch from raw finalized operands, registered multiply extension bits, real iterative divider, all exact ALTOPS formulas, branch comparison/target arithmetic and producer qualifications. Hardware-only inputs must pass strict lint in both real and ALTOPS modes.\n\nThe intended gain is removing alu_src and is_auipc operand-selection muxes from shifter launch cones, especially alu_src's five low-bit controls, while adding only five timed state bits. This can shorten shift-result EX-to-ID feedback and reduce control fanout without duplicating 32-bit ALU operands or replacing native arithmetic. Expected integer fitness gain is six percent with low confidence. It remains an estimate until fresh median vendor timing and canonical stalled throughput are measured; other feedback paths and routing may dominate. At unchanged cycles, Fmax must exceed approximately 85.286 MHz and fitness 203.1855 iter/sec to clear the 4.6 percent acceptance margin.\n\nRetain nret=1 and every top-level memory/RVFI port, exact raw source values, youngest-writer priority and x0, complete held operand stability, traps and default-illegal decoder validation, immediate recovery and accepted predictor updates, strict retirement order, progress, memory bounds and once-only effects, canonical CRCs, completion text and marker bracketing. Modify no evaluation-contract file. Run unchanged formal and retirement-cosim gates plus real/ALTOPS arithmetic and pipeline tests; score actual median Gowin Fmax times validated iterations/cycle.''','expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},'changes':[{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add only a five-bit shift_amount field to id_ex_t. Keep all existing raw operand, multiplier extension, immediate, architectural and control fields unchanged.'''},{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture shift_amount from decoded immediate versus finalized forwarded rs2 low bits on the existing acceptance edge. Preserve exact reset, flush, bubble and stall behavior and all raw RVFI source capture.'''},{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Enable the prepared-shift ALU mode and wire its data directly from in.rs1_val and its amount from the new ID/EX field. Retain ordinary operand selection for all other operations, native multiplier launch, forwarding, branch/predictor logic, divider handshake and EX/MEM behavior.'''},{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Add a default-disabled PREPARED_SHIFT parameter and the prepared data/amount inputs. Use them only for SLL/SRL/SRA when enabled; standalone mode retains a/b. Preserve the native three shift expressions, arithmetic sign extension, result case statement, every other real operation and all ALTOPS formulas.'''},{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Drive optional prepared inputs and retain every original real/ALTOPS assertion. Verify disabled mode ignores them. Cover all 32 counts, negative SRA operands and counts whose high bits are set. Exercise enabled mode via integrated pipeline tests without weakening standalone checks.'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve all original assertions. Add immediate/register SLL/SRL/SRA using EX/MEM/WB-forwarded shift data and amounts, youngest-writer conflicts, x0, counts 0/31/32/63, negative arithmetic shifts and load-to-shift dependencies. Check the new amount remains stable through dmem/division holds and clears with whole-bundle reset/flush. Verify exact RVFI source/result/order and recovery under stalls, then unchanged formal/cosim and canonical CoreMark validation before median vendor scoring.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Final candidate schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
