EX cannot supply its value: keep the current one-cycle load-use\ninterlock and bubble. On the next acceptance edge forward its current\nMEM extracted value directly into ID/EX. Genuine dmem stalls hold ID/EX\nand the memory request, preventing capture of unavailable MEM data.\nInstruction-bus unavailability still bubbles ID normally; redirect flush\nkills the younger instruction before it captures an architectural value.\nOnce captured, operands remain stable during every EX hold, independently\nof older producers draining. Never refresh held operands from later stages.\n\nPreserve division's once-only launch, o\n…[13793 of 16865 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b14886e789610aa6]…\nret=1 and exact top-level IO/RVFI, consecutive order, progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and timing markers. Add no execution stage, multiplier FSM, scheduling bubble, predictor delay, RAM or evaluation-contract change. The optimization is physically separating multiplier launch storage from general operand storage at an existing boundary.\n\nPredict six percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending the final isolated prototype measurements. Extra storage and decoded multiply-enable fanout may offset improved routing, and residual EX/predictor/ID paths may remain limiting. Do not claim a demonstrated win from this estimate. Expect identical canonical CoreMark cycle count, but score actual bracketed cycles and all three fresh Gowin placement options. Acceptance requires fitness above 203.1855 iter/sec, roughly 85.286 MHz at 4197447 cycles. Run unchanged correctness gates; all hardware multiply variants must remain exact, including dependent multiply chains and signed high halves.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
