{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_42", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r13s0',\n'title':'Balanced four-bit ALU comparison for SLT and SLTU feedback',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 timed CoreMark cycles. Balanced four-bit comparison in branch resolution was the last accepted micro-optimization; the ALU still implements SLT and SLTU with full-width native comparisons. Later paths repeatedly cross EX ALU generation and forwarding into ID operand capture. LESSONS.md is absent. Reviewed micro_opt history shows that result-select, prepared shift control, dedicated multiplier launch and raw fetch-address changes regressed. Local grouped-addition and prepared-ALU-operand prototypes also regressed. Apply the successful comparator topology to the remaining ALU magnitude comparisons, with no pipeline, mux-boundary or predictor change.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace only alu.sv's full-width ALU_SLT and ALU_SLTU comparisons with one shared balanced lexicographic network over the existing selected a/b operands. Use eight four-bit unsigned less-than/equality leaves, and combine them in three balanced levels. For each high/low pair compute lt = lt_high | (eq_high & lt_low) and eq = eq_high & eq_low. The more significant group always dominates. Use explicit constant-index wires and generated connections rather than a serial fold or another full-width subtractor.\n\nReproduce the branch comparator's topology: wire [15:1] compare_lt and compare_eq; for g=0..7, leaves [8+g] compare a[4*g +: 4] and b[4*g +: 4]; for g=1..7, combine odd high child 2*g+1 and even low child 2*g. Root compare_lt[1] is unsigned less-than. The equality root is unnecessary for SLT/SLTU and may be omitted or left for synthesis to prune; do not change the ordering or rebuild the tree as a serial recurrence. Signed less-than is (a[31] \"'^ b[31]) ? a[31] : compare_lt[1]. ALU_SLT returns {31'\"'b0,signed_lt} and ALU_SLTU returns {31'b0,compare_lt[1]}, including their immediate instruction variants through the existing a/b operand selection. Never use subtraction sign alone for signed comparison.\n\nUse exactly the current ALU-selected a and b, including immediate sign extension and AUIPC operand choice, and keep the result case/mux intact. Leave EX's existing branch comparator independent and unchanged, because it compares raw finalized rs1/rs2 rather than these selected operands. Do not share or mux the two comparator instances. No new op encodings, predecoded selector registers, operand banks, helpers or pipeline stages are part of this proposal.\n\nKeep every other operation unchanged, especially native ADD/SUB, all shifts, formatted signed multiplication, real division and every M-extension RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS expression. The balanced base SLT/SLTU logic is exact and applies in both real and ALTOPS builds. Preserve alu ports, existing ID/EX and EX/MEM boundaries, instruction latency, EX producer qualification, EX > MEM > RF forwarding, held finalized operands, divider request/sticky completion/consume, load-use and memory stalls, branch/JAL/JALR targets and traps, immediate accepted recovery and predictor policy/training.\n\nThe intended gain is a bounded four-bit local comparison plus three lexicographic levels instead of word-wide carry/borrow propagation feeding the ALU result mux and ID capture. Grouped branch comparison previously demonstrated that this shape can improve Gowin timing, but SLT/SLTU may not be limiting and routing changes can erase the benefit. Estimate six percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending vendor timing. Expect unchanged canonical CoreMark bracket cycles; score actual iterations/cycle times median vendor Fmax. Acceptance requires fitness above 203.1855 iter/sec, approximately 85.286 MHz at 4197447 cycles.\n\nProve the combinational unsigned and signed results equivalent to full-width reference comparisons for arbitrary operands. Retain all existing tests and extend arithmetic tests with each significance boundary, competing high/low group decisions, equal operands, signed extremes and deterministic random inputs. Run real and ALTOPS strict lint, existing decoder/arithmetic/pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal and retirement cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark and median Gowin timing. Preserve nret=1, exact external IO, default-illegal decoding, all traps, precise RVFI sources/results/PCs, strict retirement order, progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and MMIO brackets. Only the listed RTL and cocotb test may change.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Add one shared eight-leaf balanced four-bit magnitude comparator over existing a/b inputs. Derive signed less-than from operand signs and the unsigned root. Replace only ALU_SLT/ALU_SLTU result expressions. Preserve ports, operand/result selection, all other real/base operations and every M-extension ALTOPS formula. Use constant-index logic and keep the EX branch comparator unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain all arithmetic and latency assertions. Extend SLT/SLTU checks with independent signed/unsigned integer reference comparisons for equality, every four-bit significance boundary, conflicting significant versus lower groups, all sign combinations, INT_MIN/INT_MAX and seeded random operands. Include selected-immediate comparison coverage through unchanged pipeline tests and run existing lint/formal/cosim and canonical vendor fitness gates.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Balanced ALU comparison hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
