{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r13s0',\n'title':'Remove repeated x0 data gating from operand capture',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 timed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts at fetch readiness, passes through RF reads and ID operand capture, and ends at multiplier launch. The balanced RF trees already have a zero x0 leaf and only x1-x31 storage, but reg_file adds a full-word x0 priority mux and ID repeats x0 zeroing after forwarding. These wide gates depend on the fetched source address. LESSONS.md is absent. Reviewed micro_opt history shows that raw fetch-address routing and multiplier operand duplication regressed; integrated bypass banks also failed to clear acceptance. Keep the current read/bypass topology and remove only redundant x0 data gating.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Make x0 correctness follow from the existing zero RF leaf and nonzero producer tags, replacing repeated source-address-controlled word zeroing with WB producer qualification. This is one local Boolean datapath optimization; keep the existing flop RF words, balanced read trees, write-first bypass, separate EX/MEM forwarding selectors and pipeline boundaries.\n\nIn reg_file.sv remove the top-level if(rs?_addr==0) rs?_data=0 branches. For each port instead use if(w_en && w_addr\"'!=0 && w_addr==rs?_addr) rs?_data=w_data; else rs?_data=rs?_tree[1]. The existing x0 leaf is constant zero, and every other leaf is a stored word masked by comparison against a nonzero constant address, so the tree already returns zero for source address zero. The added common WB nonzero-destination qualification prevents the write-first bypass from overriding that zero. Preserve suppression of writes to x0, complete storage reset, constant-index word enables, all other same-cycle WB behavior and every register read result for nonzero addresses. Even w_en=1,w_addr=0,w_data!=0 must return zero when reading x0.\n\nIn id_stage.sv remove only the two final if(rs?_addr==0) rs?_final=0 statements after the current EX/MEM/RF muxes. Leave the encoded forwarding cases, their priority and every source input unchanged. The existing forward_unit excludes ex_rd=0 and mem_rd=0 explicitly, in addition to effective producer-enable guards; therefore source zero cannot select either producer, and it selects the RF result just proven zero. Do not weaken or delete any nonzero tag or effective producer qualification. Raw unused encoded source fields retain the same values and RVFI metadata. No result may enter the x0 path through forwarding, including an attempted write to x0 by ALU, load, jump or completed division.\n\nKeep the exact whole-ID/EX reset/flush/invalid-bubble clearing, accepted capture and genuine hold behavior, finalized operands and multiplier sign preparation. Do not route raw imem source addresses around masking, integrate producer banks into the RF tree, alter decoder or load-use detection, prepare speculative operands or move any bypass to EX. Keep WB writes, producer data, arithmetic/ALTOPS, native multiplication/division, branch comparison/target/traps, prediction policy, accepted recovery and training, memory handshakes, retirement and all instruction latencies unchanged.\n\nThe intended gain is removing source-address zero tests from wide RF/ID data selection while placing x0 exclusion on the much earlier registered WB destination. Removing ID'\"'s second zeroing also shortens the operand path after forwarding. The two edits implement one x0 discipline, with no additional storage and identical architectural behavior. Synthesis may already exploit some equivalences and another feedback path may dominate; estimate six percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending fresh vendor timing. Expect identical canonical timed CoreMark cycles. Score actual marker-bracketed iterations/cycle times median Gowin Fmax; acceptance requires above 203.1855 iter/sec, about 85.286 MHz at 4197447 cycles.\n\nProve the new RF output equal to the original for every read address, WB tuple and stored word, and prove source-zero forwarding cannot select EX or MEM under the unchanged unit's own tag guards. Run strict real/ALTOPS lint, existing register-file/arithmetic/decoder/pipeline suites, unchanged formal and retirement cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark and all vendor placement options. Preserve nret=1 and exact external IO, strict order, default-illegal decode, precise traps/RVFI operands/results/PCs, forward progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and both timing markers. Do not weaken any existing x0 test or other invariant; modify only the listed allowed RTL and tests.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Keep all stored words, reset, writes and masked read trees. Remove source-zero word priority branches from both read outputs and qualify each existing WB write-first hit with w_addr\"'!=0. The constant x0 tree leaf remains zero. Preserve exact read values and ignored-x0-write behavior for all WB/source tuples.'\"'''},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Remove only the redundant final rs1/rs2 x0 zeroing after the existing forwarding muxes. Keep every case, selector, effective producer/nonzero-tag guard, capture/hold/bubble behavior, raw RVFI source handling and multiplier sign preparation unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain existing checks and stress x0 reads during arbitrary nonzero-data writes to x0, writes to other registers, dual-port mixtures, repeated reset and all WB/read-address collisions. Compare against an independent architectural register model and verify x0 writes never affect storage or bypass data.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all assertions and add chains attempting ALU/load/jump/M-extension writes to x0 followed by x0 consumers on both ports. Exercise overlapping writers and independent EX/MEM/WB selections, imem/dmem stalls, load-use and division holds, checking exact RVFI zero operands, results/PCs/traps/order, once-only memory effects and progress.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('x0 operand-gating hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
