{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The shifter and x0 variants pass functional checks but still lose timing. I’m checking one final local change: capture the already-selected writeback value in MEM/WB. This should remove the load/ALU mux from WB’s register-file write and bypass paths, while sharing the mux already used by MEM forwarding."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\nbase=Path('cores/bench/generated/hyp13_wb_data_prototype')\nrtl=base/'rtl';rtl.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)\nfor p in Path('cores/bench/rtl').glob('*.sv'):\n shutil.copy2(p,rtl/p.name)\np=rtl/'core_pkg.sv';s=p.read_text().replace('    logic [31:0] read_data;      // sign/zero-extended load (for regfile write)','    logic [31:0] read_data;      // sign/zero-extended load (for regfile write)\\\\n    logic [31:0] wb_data;');p.write_text(s)\np=rtl/'mem_stage.sv';s=p.read_text().replace('      reg_q.read_data  <= load_data;','      reg_q.read_data  <= load_data;\\\\n      reg_q.wb_data    <= producer_data;');p.write_text(s)\np=rtl/'wb_stage.sv';s=p.read_text().replace('w_data = in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? in.read_data : in.alu_result;','w_data = in.wb_data;');p.write_text(s)\nprint('Prepared WB data prototype',rtl)\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nimport json\nwt=Path.cwd();base=wt/'cores/bench/generated/hyp13_wb_data_prototype'\nr=build_all(wt,base/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',base/'timing')\n(base/'timing_result.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r13s0',\n'title':'Capture the selected writeback value at the MEM/WB boundary',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 timed CoreMark cycles. Timing repeatedly exposes ID operand capture and register-file bypass as limiting. MEM already selects between extracted load data and the EX/MEM ALU result to drive producer_data, yet MEM/WB captures both words and WB repeats that selection before the high-fanout register-file write and write-first bypass paths. Capturing the existing selected MEM result removes the post-register WB mux and permits redundant result flops to be pruned. LESSONS.md is absent. Reviewed micro_opt history and local probes show that modifying EX arithmetic, operand banks or source-zero gates can regress routing; this proposal leaves those paths intact and targets the duplicated writeback selection.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one 32-bit wb_data field to mem_wb_t and capture reg_q.wb_data <= producer_data in mem_stage's existing normal capture arm. That existing combinational producer_data is exactly in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result. In wb_stage drive w_data directly from in.wb_data, replacing its separate load-versus-ALU result mux. Keep w_en = in.ctrl.reg_write && in.valid and w_addr = in.rd unchanged. This is one local mux placement and common-expression optimization with no added stage, instruction latency, bypass path or architectural behavior.\n\nInitially retain the existing MEM/WB alu_result and read_data fields and their assignments for source compatibility and test visibility. They no longer feed architectural WB once wb_data is used. Allow synthesis to prune fields unused by the timed circuit rather than routing WB back through them or adding a verification-only optimization. The intended net hardware effect is one selected result register instead of two independent result register words and a downstream mux. The selected result is also already needed for current MEM-to-ID forwarding, so share exactly that existing producer_data cone rather than duplicating another selection expression or changing its qualification.\n\nCapture wb_data under the same conditions as every existing MEM/WB payload. Whole-register reset clears it. On hold_wb retain it exactly like the current data fields while clearing only valid; do not expose a new retirement or write enable. On a normal edge capture the result of that edge's own EX/MEM instruction, including bubble payload behavior. Preserve trap and load-alignment controls, read/write suppression, byte/halfword extraction and sign extension. A load only becomes a valid WB result when the existing dmem handshake allows MEM to advance. Do not qualify data capture with producer_w_en, because zero-destination, trapping and bubble payloads must retain the existing capture protocol even when their architectural write enable is false.\n\nLeave reg_file and its balanced flop read trees, x0 priority, WB write-first bypass, and current EX > MEM > RF forwarding topology unchanged. Preserve the current MEM producer enable/data semantics for ID and every EX operand, opcode, result, branch and multiplier path. Keep ID/EX whole-bundle clearing and held finalized operands, load-use and both bus interlocks, sticky divider completion and consume, native/ALTOPS arithmetic, branch/JAL/JALR targets and traps, prediction policy and immediate accepted recovery/training unchanged. RVFI rd_wdata uses the unchanged WB output and must equal the original selected result for every retirement. All other RVFI fields retain their exact definitions.\n\nThe expected gain is removing a wide mem_to_reg-controlled mux between MEM/WB flops and RF writes/bypass, shortening the WB-to-ID launch path and reducing duplicated result storage and routing. Added MEM result-register fanout and other EX feedback limits can erase this benefit. Estimate six percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending fresh median vendor timing. Expect identical canonical CoreMark cycles, but score actual marker-bracketed iterations/cycle times Gowin median Fmax. Acceptance requires fitness above 203.1855 iter/sec, approximately 85.286 MHz at 4197447 cycles.\n\nVerify the new registered value equals the original WB mux value on every valid MEM/WB transaction, including prolonged dmem holds and older WB draining. Retain all existing assertions and run strict real/ALTOPS lint, arithmetic/decoder/pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal, retirement cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark and all vendor placement options. Preserve nret=1 and exact external IO, default-illegal decode, all traps, precise RVFI operands/results/PCs, consecutive orders, progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and start/stop markers. Change only the listed allowed RTL and cocotb test.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add 32-bit wb_data to mem_wb_t. Retain all existing fields and other bundle definitions for compatibility; synthesis may prune words no longer used by timed consumers.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Capture existing producer_data into reg_q.wb_data in the normal MEM/WB capture arm. Preserve full reset, payload retention while hold_wb clears valid, all existing memory/forwarding/trap logic and exact acceptance conditions. Do not gate capture with producer_w_en.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from registered in.wb_data instead of selecting read_data versus alu_result. Preserve w_en, w_addr and the module interface; keep all top-level RVFI and RF connections unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain existing checks and add WB/RF collision sequences alternating ALU and byte/half/word load results, signed loads, consecutive writes to one rd, x0, misaligned/trapping loads and same-edge dual-source WB bypass. Exercise imem/dmem stalls, load-use, divider waits and older WB draining. Compare exact independent architectural RVFI operands/results/order/PCs, once-only effects and bounded progress; assert selected MEM/WB data aligns with the accepted instruction.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Selected-WB hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r13s0',\n'title':'Capture the selected writeback value at the MEM/WB boundary',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 timed CoreMark cycles. Timing repeatedly exposes ID operand capture and register-file bypass as limiting. MEM already selects between extracted load data and the EX/MEM ALU result to drive producer_data, yet MEM/WB captures both words and WB repeats that selection before the high-fanout register-file write and write-first bypass paths. Capturing the existing selected MEM result removes the post-register WB mux and permits redundant result flops to be pruned. LESSONS.md is absent. Reviewed micro_opt history and local probes show that modifying EX arithmetic, operand banks or source-zero gates can regress routing; this proposal leaves those paths intact and targets the duplicated writeback selection.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one 32-bit wb_data field to mem_wb_t and capture reg_q.wb_data <= producer_data in mem_stage's existing normal capture arm. That existing combinational producer_data is exactly in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result. In wb_stage drive w_data directly from in.wb_data, replacing its separate load-versus-ALU result mux. Keep w_en = in.ctrl.reg_write && in.valid and w_addr = in.rd unchanged. This is one local mux placement and common-expression optimization with no added stage, instruction latency, bypass path or architectural behavior.\n\nInitially retain the existing MEM/WB alu_result and read_data fields and their assignments for source compatibility and test visibility. They no longer feed architectural WB once wb_data is used. Allow synthesis to prune fields unused by the timed circuit rather than routing WB back through them or adding a verification-only optimization. The intended net hardware effect is one selected result register instead of two independent result register words and a downstream mux. The selected result is also already needed for current MEM-to-ID forwarding, so share exactly that existing producer_data cone rather than duplicating another selection expression or changing its qualification.\n\nCapture wb_data under the same conditions as every existing MEM/WB payload. Whole-register reset clears it. On hold_wb retain it exactly like the current data fields while clearing only valid; do not expose a new retirement or write enable. On a normal edge capture the result of that edge's own EX/MEM instruction, including bubble payload behavior. Preserve trap and load-alignment controls, read/write suppression, byte/halfword extraction and sign extension. A load only becomes a valid WB result when the existing dmem handshake allows MEM to advance. Do not qualify data capture with producer_w_en, because zero-destination, trapping and bubble payloads must retain the existing capture protocol even when their architectural write enable is false.\n\nLeave reg_file and its balanced flop read trees, x0 priority, WB write-first bypass, and current EX > MEM > RF forwarding topology unchanged. Preserve the current MEM producer enable/data semantics for ID and every EX operand, opcode, result, branch and multiplier path. Keep ID/EX whole-bundle clearing and held finalized operands, load-use and both bus interlocks, sticky divider completion and consume, native/ALTOPS arithmetic, branch/JAL/JALR targets and traps, prediction policy and immediate accepted recovery/training unchanged. RVFI rd_wdata uses the unchanged WB output and must equal the original selected result for every retirement. All other RVFI fields retain their exact definitions.\n\nThe expected gain is removing a wide mem_to_reg-controlled mux between MEM/WB flops and RF writes/bypass, shortening the WB-to-ID launch path and reducing duplicated result storage and routing. Added MEM result-register fanout and other EX feedback limits can erase this benefit. Estimate six percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending fresh median vendor timing. Expect identical canonical CoreMark cycles, but score actual marker-bracketed iterations/cycle times Gowin median Fmax. Acceptance requires fitness above 203.1855 iter/sec, approximately 85.286 MHz at 4197447 cycles.\n\nVerify the new registered value equals the original WB mux value on every valid MEM/WB transaction, including prolonged dmem holds and older WB draining. Retain all existing assertions and run strict real/ALTOPS lint, arithmetic/decoder/pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal, retirement cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark and all vendor placement options. Preserve nret=1 and exact external IO, default-illegal decode, all traps, precise RVFI operands/results/PCs, consecutive orders, progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and start/stop markers. Change only the listed allowed RTL and cocotb test.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add 32-bit wb_data to mem_wb_t. Retain all existing fields and other bundle definitions for compatibility; synthesis may prune words no longer used by timed consumers.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Capture existing producer_data into reg_q.wb_data in the normal MEM/WB capture arm. Preserve full reset, payload retention while hold_wb clears valid, all existing memory/forwarding/trap logic and exact acceptance conditions. Do not gate capture with producer_w_en.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from registered in.wb_data instead of selecting read_data versus alu_result. Preserve w_en, w_addr and the module interface; keep all top-level RVFI and RF connections unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain existing checks and add WB/RF collision sequences alternating ALU and byte/half/word load results, signed loads, consecutive writes to one rd, x0, misaligned/trapping loads and same-edge dual-source WB bypass. Exercise imem/dmem stalls, load-use, divider waits and older WB draining. Compare exact independent architectural RVFI operands/results/order/PCs, once-only effects and bounded progress; assert selected MEM/WB data aligns with the accepted instruction.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Selected-WB hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Selected-WB hypothesis is schema-valid\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
