{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r3s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-001-r3s0\ntitle: \\\"Share one native DSP multiplier across RV32M signedness variants\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 117.38 at a median 49.269 MHz. Its worst path is\n  26.661 ns through register-file reads, operand muxes, the EX multiplier\n  and result selection. A fresh Gowin build reproduced the reported\n  place-option-0 Fmax of 46.803 MHz and explicitly identified a 3.780 ns\n  multiplier cell on that path. alu.sv computes three independent\n  full-width products for signed/signed, unsigned/unsigned and\n  signed/unsigned variants, and the timed design consumes six DSP blocks.\n  Sharing the arithmetic can reduce operand fanout, DSP placement span\n  and product-selection logic without adding an instruction cycle.\n  No LESSONS.md exists. The prior micro_opt borrow-lookahead divider\n  regressed, while redirect-mask removal gained only 3.63 percent and\n  is already incorporated into the champion's predictor recovery.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the three real multiplication networks in alu.sv with one\n  direct, combinational signed 33-by-33 multiplication, dynamically\n  extending each 32-bit operand according to the multiply operation.\n  Keep all module ports, pipeline registers, scheduling and instruction\n  latencies unchanged. This is one local multiplier-sharing optimization.\n\n  Let sign_a=(op==ALU_MULH || op==ALU_MULHSU), and let\n  sign_b=(op==ALU_MULH). Declare mul_a and mul_b as signed [32:0] and\n  mul_product as signed [65:0]. Assign\n  mul_a=\"'$signed({sign_a && a[31], a}) and\n  mul_b=$signed({sign_b && b[31], b}), then compute\n  mul_product=mul_a*mul_b once. For MUL and MULHU both extensions are\n  zero; for MULH both are sign extensions; for MULHSU only A is sign\n  extended. This represents unsigned values through 0xffffffff as\n  positive signed 33-bit values, while preserving every signed operand.\n  Select mul_product[31:0] for MUL and the same mul_product[63:32] slice\n  for MULH, MULHU and MULHSU. Product bits [65:64] are unused. The low\n  32-bit product is independent of signedness, and the high 32-bit slices\n  are exact RV32M results. Let Gowin infer its native large DSP multiplier\n  directly from this explicitly sized signed expression. The operation\n  selects input extension bits and the output half, so the three separate\n  products and their distinct high-half result paths disappear.\n\n  Put the real multiplier declarations and assignments under\n  `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Preserve the four existing multiplication\n  ALTOPS expressions exactly in their existing operation cases. Keep\n  helper logic in alu.sv so source lists require no changes. Preserve\n  all base integer operations, divider wiring and sticky handshake,\n  predictor behavior, decoder legality, misalignment traps, memory-ready\n  handling, forwarding, reset and the exact nret=1 IO/RVFI contract.\n  Modify only the listed ALU and unit-test files.\n\n  An isolated prototype using these exact expressions and the unchanged\n  rest of the champion RTL completed all three vendor placement options:\n  Fmax values were 53.382, 53.083 and 53.083 MHz, median 53.083 MHz.\n  DSP count fell from six to two, logic from 3280 to 3205, with unchanged\n  registers, LUT-RAM and BSRAM counts. Median Fmax increased 7.74 percent.\n  Since no pipeline or handshake cycle changes, useful iterations/cycle\n  should remain unchanged, implying roughly 126.47 fitness if confirmed\n  by the canonical stalled CoreMark run. This is a timing measurement\n  and a projected fitness, not a completed evaluator result. Estimate\n  an 8 percent fitness improvement, exceeding the 4.6 percent acceptance\n  threshold; remaining risks are implementation details changing the\n  mapped circuit and any arithmetic or formal regression.\n\n  Retain every existing arithmetic assertion and add deterministic\n  boundary/random checks against exact Python integer multiplication\n  for all four multiply variants. Run the ALU suite, existing formal\n  gate and retirement cosimulation, including dependencies through the\n  existing forwarding paths and memory stalls. Confirm the unchanged\n  canonical 2K CoreMark CRCs and marker-bracketed cycle count, then score\n  median Gowin Fmax times iterations/cycle. Keep all evaluation and\n  contract files unchanged; RVFI-only simplification is not the target.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace mul_ss, mul_uu and mul_su with signed [32:0] mul_a/mul_b\n      and signed [65:0] mul_product. Derive sign_a for MULH/MULHSU and\n      sign_b for MULH, explicitly concatenate each selected sign bit\n      with its 32-bit operand, and perform one signed multiply. Select\n      its low half for MUL and its shared high half for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.\n      Guard real-product logic from ALTOPS builds, retaining all existing\n      substitute formulas, ALU ports, combinational latency, base integer\n      operations and divider wiring. No additional module is required.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing vectors and divider handshake assertions.\n      Add exact Python-integer reference checks for all four multiply\n      operations with deterministic random 32-bit pairs and a boundary\n      cross-product covering 0, 1, -1, INT_MIN, INT_MAX, 0x0000ffff,\n      0x00010000, 0x0001ffff, 0xffff0000, 0x7fff0000, 0x80000001 and\n      neighbors of powers of two. Stress unsigned values above INT_MAX,\n      every operand-sign combination, carry across bits 16 and 32,\n      overflow modulo 2''^64, and successive operation changes with the\n      same operands. Check exact low and high RV32M result bits.\n'\"''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated shared-native-multiplier hypothesis passes schema validation.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
