{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_101","type":"agent_message","text":"Preparing both PC destinations regresses timing, so I’ve ruled it out. The final hypothesis will predecode ALU result selection at ID/EX and share selectors for identical high-product and divider results. A broader version reached 82.640 MHz; the reduced selector is being timed. The gain remains uncertain."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_102", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r7s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r7s0',\n 'title':'Register grouped one-hot ALU result selection before execute',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iterations/second at 81.537 MHz with\n4197447 canonical stalled CoreMark bracket cycles. Its four-bit balanced\nbranch comparator shortened EX feedback, and prior multiplier operand\nformatting improved timing by moving operation-dependent preparation\nbefore the existing execute boundary. The remaining EX-to-ID producer\npath still decodes a five-bit ALU operation before selecting a result.\nLESSONS.md is absent. The micro_opt history has not registered grouped\nALU result-selection enables. Extra fetch/decode stages and registered\nrecovery failed; this proposal preserves stages and latency.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Predecode the hardware ALU result family at the existing ID/EX capture\nedge, then use registered one-hot enables and a balanced masked OR\nnetwork in EX. Preserve the existing five-bit alu_op for divider\noperation capture and exact ALTOPS semantics. Add a fourteen-bit\nalu_select field to id_ex_t, captured from the unchanged validated\ndecoder's dec_alu_op on the same edge as controls and finalized operands.\nThe complete bundle still resets and flushes to zero and holds unchanged\nunder every existing stall. Do not add stages or scheduling changes.\n\nUse fourteen mutually exclusive result groups: ADD, SUB, AND, OR, XOR,\nSLT, SLTU, SLL, SRL, SRA, LUI, low multiply, high multiply, and completed\ndivide/remainder. Bits 0 through 11 correspond to ALU operations 0 through\n11. All MULH/MULHU/MULHSU operations select bit 12 because their hardware\nresult is the same shared mul_product[63:32]; existing registered operand\nsign bits already determine the signedness. All DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU\noperations select bit 13 because their actual result comes from the same\nsticky divider result register; the divider still captures the exact op.\nGenerate these grouped enables in ID, avoiding a later OR of three\nmultiply selectors or four divide selectors after ID/EX.\n\nKeep native arithmetic expressions, operand routing, formatted native\nmultiplier ports and two-DSP sharing, true restoring divider and its\nlaunch/consume protocol unchanged. Mask each of the fourteen candidate\n32-bit results with its registered group enable and combine them in an\nexplicit balanced OR tree with constant indices. Pad unused leaves with\nzero if needed. A zero selector returns zero, preserving bubble safety.\nDo not re-encode these enables into a priority mux, insert operation\ncomparisons on the hardware result-selection path, or compute separate\nhigh-half products/divider results. Keep the current div_done qualification\nso unfinished arithmetic is never supplied as a result.\n\nAdd a default-disabled REGISTERED_SELECT parameter to alu. Standalone\nALU tests/users keep decoding op locally with the same grouping; only\nEX instantiates the prepared-selector mode and supplies in.alu_select.\nKeep out=0 for unsupported ALU operation values. In ALTOPS mode, preserve\neach multiply variant's exact original formula despite grouped high-half\nselection: select its distinct substitute using the retained exact op\ninside the formal-only branch. All division substitutes remain in the\nunchanged divider and use its original sticky handshake. Formal-only\nselection is pruned from the real timed circuit. Keep hardware-only input\nannotations compatible with strict lint in both configurations.\n\nAn isolated nineteen-selector prototype, retaining one enable per original\nALU operation, measures 82.640 MHz on Gowin placement option one with\n4480 logic cells and 1876 registers. Its 1.35 percent Fmax gain is below\nthe acceptance threshold and does not establish a win. The proposed\nfourteen-group implementation removes redundant registered selects and\npost-register combination of identical hardware result sources. Estimate\na six percent fitness improvement with low confidence. Remaining native\nadder, branch feedback, register-file and routing paths may limit the\nbenefit. At unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed about 85.29 MHz.\nDo not present a single-placement result as a scored median.\n\nPreserve every current architectural behavior: EX/MEM/WB forwarding and\npriority, captured source/RVFI values, JAL/JALR links, AUIPC and full-width\naddresses, balanced branch comparisons, direction/target recovery,\npredictor policy/training, illegal and misalignment traps, memory\nbackpressure and nret=1 IO/retirement. Keep all existing assertions and\nchecks, including zero bubbles and full-bundle stability under holds.\nVerify all fourteen groups, all M-extension variants, youngest-writer\nsources, x0, load dependencies and reset/recovery during stalled execution.\nRun real arithmetic vectors, strict real/ALTOPS lint, unchanged formal and\nretirement cosim, then canonical stalled CoreMark with exact CRC/UART,\nbounds and marker-bracketed cycles. Score actual median Gowin Fmax times\nmeasured iterations/cycle; modify only allowed RTL and cocotb tests.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add a fourteen-bit alu_select field to id_ex_t while preserving exact alu_op, all existing metadata and nret=1.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture one-hot result groups from dec_alu_op at the existing acceptance\nedge: base operations and MUL use their existing indices, all high\nmultiply variants use bit 12, and all divide/remainder variants bit 13.\nKeep reset/flush/invalid zeroing and full-bundle holds unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Instantiate alu in prepared-selector mode and drive its grouped selects\ndirectly from ID/EX. Preserve every operand connection, producer enable,\nredirect, trap, predictor update and EX/MEM acceptance rule.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Add default-compatible prepared-selector support and a balanced masked\nOR across fourteen unchanged arithmetic result sources. Share high\nproduct and divider-result selection before ID/EX. Keep the exact\noperation-dependent multiply ALTOPS formulas in the formal-only branch,\nall native arithmetic and divider protocols, and unsupported-op zero.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain every existing exact arithmetic/operand-isolation assertion.\nCheck default and prepared selection modes across all operations,\nshared high-product/divider groups, zero/unsupported selects, real M\ncorner cases and exact ALTOPS variants without weakening assertions.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Keep all existing RVFI, trap, order, memory-effect, reset/flush and\nheld-bundle checks. Verify grouped selects remain paired with controls,\noperands and multiplier sign bits through load-use, division, both bus\nstalls and recovery. Exercise each group and all eight M operations\nwith competing EX/MEM/WB writers and immediately dependent consumers.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=76))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261005-001-r7s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nprint('Final hypothesis schema validation passed:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
