{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r9s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r9s0',\n 'title':'Select architectural writeback data before the MEM/WB register',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 194.25 iter/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4197447 canonical stalled CoreMark bracket cycles. Timing alternates between EX-to-ID forwarding and IF/RF/ID operand capture. WB currently selects separately registered load and ALU words before broadcasting write data to all RF write ports and both WB read bypasses. Moving this selection before the existing MEM/WB register removes that late mux and permits unused duplicate load-result registers to be pruned. LESSONS.md is absent. Relevant prior micro_opt attempts found native multiplier sharing and balanced branch comparisons useful, but grouped ALU result selection regressed and branch predicate factoring stayed below acceptance. This proposal preserves the current pipeline and targets WB data routing rather than those unsuccessful control rewrites.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Prepare the final architectural register-write value in MEM and capture it at the existing MEM/WB edge, then drive WB data directly from that registered word. The current MEM producer_data already implements the required selection: ctrl.mem_to_reg ? extracted_load_data : EX/MEM.alu_result. Reuse that expression for the MEM/WB writeback payload. WB continues to derive write enable from registered validity and effective reg_write, and destination from the registered rd, but no longer selects between independently registered read_data and alu_result.\n\nFor a minimal implementation, repurpose MEM/WB.alu_result as this final writeback word, updating its comment to reflect the meaning; keep EX/MEM.alu_result unchanged. Preserve the existing MEM/WB.read_data capture if desired for internal observability, but no timed hardware consumer should require that duplicate field. Do not add another simultaneously timed final-writeback register while retaining both old WB result sources. All RVFI raw memory data, masks, addresses, raw source operands, PC/next PC, instruction, controls and order remain independent and unchanged. Arithmetic and jump link values still come from the current EX/MEM producer, while loads capture the existing sign/zero extension exactly.\n\nRetain reset-zeroing and all MEM/WB hold behavior. On dmem stall, clear only valid and retain the selected word with the rest of the old payload; WB validity still prevents repeated writes/retirements. An advancing MEM instruction captures its own selected result even when nonwriting or invalid, as the present payload update already does; introduce no late validity/width/ready enable for its data flops. The current MEM-to-ID producer path, its misalignment-qualified write enable, load-use bubble, ID capture and source forwarding priorities are unchanged. Preserve every other pipeline register and latency, branch predictor/recovery/training, multiplier operand launch and exact RV32IM/ALTOPS arithmetic, divider sticky protocol, decoder default-illegal and validated legality, misaligned-access suppression, nret=1/top-level IO, bus-handshake acceptance, full cleared bubbles, held operands, retirement order/progress, memory bounds, canonical CRCs/UART validation and timing markers.\n\nEstimate a six percent fitness gain with low confidence before final median timing. The intended benefit is removing the wide post-WB mux from the RF write and read-bypass fanout and reducing duplicate timed WB storage, while moving the selection onto the already existing MEM producer path. A MEM-to-WB or EX feedback path may still cap Fmax. Expect identical canonical CoreMark cycles, but use actual bracket cycles and fresh median Gowin Fmax. At 4197447 cycles the acceptance gate requires fitness above 203.1855 iter/sec and median Fmax above approximately 85.29 MHz. Modify only the listed allowed RTL and cocotb files, and run all unchanged correctness gates.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Capture the existing MEM architectural producer_data into the MEM/WB writeback-result word in the ordinary advancing arm. Preserve the exact load extractor, all raw RVFI memory fields, metadata, control/traps, reset and hold-valid behavior; keep the MEM-to-ID producer path unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'Drive w_data directly from the registered final writeback-result word. Retain the exact registered-valid/reg_write write enable and rd routing, with no new WB clocking, ready qualification or side effects.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Document that the MEM/WB result payload now contains the selected architectural writeback value. Keep EX/MEM.alu_result semantics, bundle/IO shapes, control fields and all other payloads unchanged.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain every existing architectural and internal hold/bubble assertion. Add load/ALU/JAL/JALR alternation at WB, all signed/unsigned byte/halfword/word loads, WB-only dependencies on both ID ports, competing EX/MEM/WB writers and x0. Exercise dmem holds while an older WB result drains, load misalignment and wrong-path suppression; check exact RVFI values and consecutive once-only retirement. Run unchanged unit/lint/formal and full retirement cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/bracketing validation, and fresh median vendor scoring.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Saved and validated writeback-selection hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
