y hold.\n\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, compute the exact original per-operation\n  substitute F from the finalized architectural a/b operands and export\n  {F,F} as the raw product. Register and select it through the same\n  pipeline and dependency timing as the real product, so both low/high\n  variants return F. Do not change any formula, formal wrapper, check,\n  configuration or depth. Real builds use the native signed multiplication.\n  Adapt internal ALU observations to its exported raw product; preserve\n  every existing arithmetic reference assertion and all corner vectors.\n\n  This is one structural change to multiplication execution boundaries and\n  the required producer-latency interlock. Estimate eight percent fitness\n  gain with medium confidence pending vendor measurement. The r10 measured\n  dependency cost was only 35482 extra cycles, approximately 0.845 percent;\n  use fresh actual bracket cycles rather than assuming that count here.\n  At 4232929 cycles, acceptance requires median Fmax above about 86.007\n  MHz and fitness strictly above 203.1855 iter/sec. The main risks are DSP\n  register inference, product-register routing and remaining branch/predictor\n  feedback. Timing alone or small placement changes cannot establish a win.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, exact top-level memory/RVFI IO, legality and traps,\n  RV32IM arithmetic, forward progress, consecutive RVFI order, memory\n  bounds, canonical CRCs, completion text and timing markers. Run unchanged\n  lint, arithmetic/pipeline tests, single-channel formal and retirement\n  cosimulation, then canonical stalled CoreMark and fresh median Gowin\n  timing. Change only allowed RTL and cocotb tests; no contract relaxation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a 64-bit raw multiplication payload to ex_mem_t. Keep every\n      existing architectural control, operand, prediction and RVFI field;\n      MEM/WB continues carrying the selected 32-bit architectural result.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the low 64 bits of the existing shared signed 33x33 native\n      product separately, with the same prepared operand inputs. Remove\n      multiply variants from ordinary out. Under ALTOPS export {F,F} for\n      the exact existing per-op formulas. Preserve base ALU and divider.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the native raw product in separate unreset payload flops\n      on \"'!stall and retain it on dmem holds. Pair it with unchanged EX/MEM\n      metadata/validity; override the output bundle'\"'s product field with\n      these flops. Exclude all multiplies from current-EX forwarding.\n      Preserve ordinary results, divider, recovery, training and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the registered low or high product half by the saved op,\n      then use it for MEM forwarding and MEM/WB.alu_result. Introduce no\n      correction or reconstruction arithmetic. Preserve load extraction,\n      byte masks, memory outputs, traps and once-only retirement behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Qualify valid legal multiply operations as unavailable current-EX\n      producers for the existing load-style dependency interlock. Preserve\n      conservative source matching, x0 exclusion, dmem/divider/recovery\n      priorities, existing forward_unit and exact nret=1 top-level IO.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Observe the exported raw product's selected low/high half for MUL\n      tests while retaining all exact signedness, boundary, random and\n      prepared-operand isolation assertions. Check exact ALTOPS substitutes\n      through {F,F}; retain every ordinary ALU and divider assertion.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update only explicit multiply-dependency latency assumptions; retain\n      all architectural assertions. Add independent back-to-back multiplies,\n      immediate/spaced consumers of every variant, dependent chains and\n      overlapping same-rd EX/MEM/WB writes. Cover ALU, branch, JALR, load/\n      store address/data and division consumers, x0, real dmem holds,\n      imem stalls, stale predictions, wrong-path products and reset.\n      Require one bubble for direct dependencies and none for independent\n      consumers, exact RVFI fields/order, stable held requests/products,\n      once-only memory effects and bounded forward progress.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r11s1.yaml'\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(open(p)),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Revised structural hypothesis is schema-valid\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
