{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwanted={'hyp-20261005-002-r6s1','hyp-20261005-002-r7s1','hyp-20261005-002-r4s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in wanted:\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print(d['id']); print(h['hypothesis'])\n  print('notes:', d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r4s1\nMove operand forwarding into ID and latch fully resolved architectural\nrs1/rs2 values in ID/EX. Execute reads only these registered operands;\nremove its EX/MEM and MEM/WB source-forwarding muxes and comparisons.\nBack this boundary with an explicit flop register file whose constant\nword indices and masked-OR read networks prevent Gowin from absorbing\noperand capture into BSRAM. These are parts of one structural change:\ncomplete register access and bypass routing before the execute boundary.\nKeep instruction latency, issue width, load-use bubble, predictor design,\nsame-cycle EX recovery, native multiplier and iterative divider intact.\n\nIn ID, choose the youngest older writer first: the current EX instruction's\ncombinational architectural result, then the current MEM instruction's\nresult, then the register-file read with its current-WB bypass.\nEX exposes its ALU result, PC+4 for JAL/JALR, or completed divider result;\nMEM exposes the sign/zero-extended load value or its ALU result. Match\nsource addresses against valid producer destination tags with effective\nreg_write and nonzero-destination qualifications. Suppress EX\nbranch/jump-misalignment and MEM access-misalignment writes before\nadvertising a producer. Preserve x0 priority and the current RVFI source\nvalues, including the handling of unused encoded source fields.\n\nCapture finalized operands only when ID/EX actually accepts. A load still\nin EX cannot supply its value: keep the current one-cycle load-use\ninterlock and bubble. On the next acceptance edge forward its current\nMEM extracted value directly into ID/EX. Genuine dmem stalls hold ID/EX\nand the memory request, preventing capture of unavailable MEM data.\nInstruction-bus unavailability still bubbles ID normally; redirect flush\nkills the younger instruction before it captures an architectural value.\nOnce captured, operands remain stable during every EX hold, independently\nof older producers draining. Never refresh held operands from later stages.\n\nPreserve division's once-only launch, op\n…[12026 of 15098 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ce017efa63ea97f6]…\nemonstrated champion replacement. At unchanged cycles, median Fmax\nmust exceed about 85.29 MHz and fitness must exceed 203.1855 iter/sec\nto clear that gate. Smaller gains must not be accepted as a win.\n\nThe four-bank storage topology is the strongest isolated structural\noption found in this investigation. Two sixteen-row banks, eight\nfour-row banks, alternate bank indexing and folding validity/write\nbypass into masked bank terms all timed worse. Moving RF commit earlier or preparing general ALU\noperands earlier also timed worse. Retain the specified four-bank\nasynchronous layout rather than combining those unsuccessful changes.\nExtra read replication, bank-output muxes, valid-bit qualification,\nthe remaining EX-to-ID path and the unfinished register-state proof\nare the practical limits. Final implementation must complete every\nunchanged correctness gate and obtain fresh median vendor timing and\ncanonical stalled marker-bracketed throughput. A timing or area gain\nwithout those checks is insufficient.\n\nnotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
