e load-use\ninterlock and bubble. On the next acceptance edge forward its current\nMEM extracted value directly into ID/EX. Genuine dmem stalls hold ID/EX\nand the memory request, preventing capture of unavailable MEM data.\nInstruction-bus unavailability still bubbles ID normally; redirect flush\nkills the younger instruction before it captures an architectural value.\nOnce captured, operands remain stable during every EX hold, independently\nof older producers draining. Never refresh held operands from later stages.\n\nPreserve division's once-only launch, op\n…[11490 of 14562 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9fab305c3ddefd71]…\n1.5 percent\ncycle cost, median Fmax above approximately 82.40 MHz clears the 4.6 percent\nacceptance threshold. Predict an eight percent fitness improvement with\nlow confidence pending final vendor timing: remaining MEM-load extraction,\nEX-to-ID forwarding and predictor-update cones may still cap Fmax.\n\nValidate unchanged legality, arithmetic and retirement-cosim gates, plus\npipeline tests for paired saved controls/immediates, consume/refill,\nfull/empty holds, buffered decode with imemReady low, load-use, division,\nboth buses stalled, reset and wrong-path store/divider squashing. Run the\nunchanged nret=1 formal checks. Score canonical CRC-validated stalled\nCoreMark bracket cycles times median Gowin Fmax. Acceptance requires more\nthan 193.42 iter/sec; timing or area improvement alone is insufficient.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/mul_product_65_s1', 'slack_ns': -9.008, 'arrival_ns': 19.082, 'logic_cells': 38, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_predictor', 'cpu/u_id']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
