{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Register accepted predictor-training transactions outside branch recovery\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 126.46 with median Gowin Fmax 53.083 MHz and\n  4,197,447 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path is\n  24.027 ns across 44 cells, from the register file through ID, EX,\n  predictor, fetch and ID again. The previous registered branch-recovery\n  attempt increased CoreMark cycles to 4,850,577 and lost fitness; the\n  registered partial-product multiplier increased cycles to 4,478,376\n  and scored only 123.32. These results favor a control-path register\n  boundary that adds no execute or recovery latency. LESSONS.md is absent;\n  none of the three previous structural attempts isolated predictor\n  training from speculative lookup while retaining same-cycle EX recovery.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a one-entry, fully pipelined training-transaction register between\n  accepted EX instructions and the existing branch predictor table update.\n  This is one structural retiming change: keep the 64-entry tagged predictor,\n  lookup indexing, target representation, saturating counters, prediction\n  policy, combinational multiplier, iterative divider and EX recovery point.\n  Each cycle can capture one new accepted instruction's complete training\n  packet and apply the previous packet, so steady-state training bandwidth\n  remains one instruction per cycle without backpressure to execution.\n\n  Capture update_en, update_pc, update_branch, update_jump, update_legal,\n  update_taken and update_target on the EX acceptance edge. Only the\n  registered packet may drive table write enables, addresses, payloads,\n  target-range checks, update-tag matching and next-counter computation.\n  Compute matching and counter saturation against the table state when\n  applying the packet, rather than caching an obsolete pre-update counter.\n  Consecutive transactions to the same index must apply in program order.\n  Do not bypass live EX inputs into lookup or table-update logic. The a\n…[3571 of 6643 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ecf08f1de97c7f00]…\ng all prediction, tag, counter and invalidation\n      assertions. Add back-to-back same-PC and colliding-index updates,\n      branch/jump replacement, delayed ordinary/trapping invalidation,\n      bubble packets, old-row lookup on application, and reset while a\n      valid packet is pending. Assert sustained one-packet-per-cycle\n      training and absence of live-input-to-lookup bypass.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add tight taken loops, consecutive control transfers and forwarded\n      branch/JALR inputs with both buses stalled around capture/application.\n      Check that held EX instructions enqueue once, pending accepted\n      training drains during later holds or redirects, reset cancels it,\n      and wrong-path instructions never train or produce memory effects.\n      Retain exact RVFI operand/result, PC-continuity, consecutive-order,\n      once-only memory and bounded-progress checks. Run unchanged formal,\n      retirement cosim and stalled CoreMark/Gowin fitness evaluation.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
