ninterlock and bubble. On the next acceptance edge forward its current\nMEM extracted value directly into ID/EX. Genuine dmem stalls hold ID/EX\nand the memory request, preventing capture of unavailable MEM data.\nInstruction-bus unavailability still bubbles ID normally; redirect flush\nkills the younger instruction before it captures an architectural value.\nOnce captured, operands remain stable during every EX hold, independently\nof older producers draining. Never refresh held operands from later stages.\n\nPreserve division's once-only launch, op\n…[6678 of 9750 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:91b7a9e94ee3a254]…\n. These are\nisolated prototype results, not a completed orchestrator evaluation.\nPredict an integer 8 percent improvement with high confidence in\nthe measured timing/throughput effect. The final implementation must\nstill rerun the unchanged formal and evaluation gates and obtain\nfresh median vendor timing; another implementation's mapping may vary.\nAt unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed about 74.964 MHz and\nfitness must exceed 178.59404 iter/sec to clear the acceptance gate.\n\nValidate strict lint, exact real M-extension results, all existing\npipeline/decoder/register-file tests and unchanged formal/cosim gates.\nVerify the formatted bits use the correct youngest forwarded source,\nstay paired through dmem/divider holds and reset/flush correctly.\nRun canonical 2K CoreMark with both bus-stall flags, all required\nCRCs/UART validation, memory bounds and start/stop markers, then\nmeasure median Gowin Fmax times actual bracketed iterations/cycle.\nModify only allowed RTL/tests and preserve all hard invariants.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
