{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"agent_message","text":"The operand-selection prototype moves the critical path into instruction decode and register reads. I’m refining the same pipeline partition: register decoded controls and immediates in the elastic fetch slot, then use the following stage for register reads, forwarding, and ALU operand capture."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r6s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Separate instruction decode from registered operand collection'\nh['motivation']='''The champion scores 184.91 iter/sec at median Gowin Fmax 77.615 MHz,\nwith 4197447 stalled CoreMark bracket cycles. There is no separate\nfetch/decode register: instruction masking, decode, immediate generation,\nregister reads, forwarding and operand capture share one acceptance path.\nThe latest worst path also crosses EX, predictor logic and ID operand\ncapture. A clean pipeline partition can shorten the operand-capture cone\nwithout globally delaying ALU producers or multiplying execution latency.\n\nLESSONS.md is absent. Prior structural experiments show a 35.02 percent\naccepted gain from finalized operands before EX, while registered\nmultipliers and delayed recovery failed to beat their contemporary\nchampions. Isolated probes in this proposal turn confirm that another\nresult stage adds about 20 percent CoreMark cycles, and moving branch\ncomparison into ID lengthens arithmetic-to-comparison feedback. A raw\ninstruction buffer and preformatted ALU operands each expose remaining\ncapture paths when tried separately. The selected hypothesis partitions\ninstruction decode and operand collection into distinct registered stages.\n'''\nh['hypothesis']='''Introduce one elastic fetch/decode register and move instruction decoding\nand immediate generation before it. The registered transaction contains\nPC, original instruction, decoded ctrl_t, immediate, prediction metadata\nand validity. Decode the actual imemData with the existing strict decoder;\nits is_illegal default and positively validated opcode/funct matches stay\nunchanged. The following operand-collection stage reads only this saved\ninstruction and saved control/immediate fields, rather than decoding\nlive instruction-memory data again. There must be no empty-slot bypass\nfrom imemData into register addresses or execute-operand capture.\n\nThe operand stage retains current youngest EX > MEM > RF forwarding and\nWB write-first behavior. It captures finalized raw rs1/rs2 for branches,\nstores, multiplication and RVFI. Also capture the general ALU inputs on\nthe same ID/EX acceptance: alu_a is the saved PC for AUIPC, otherwise\nfinalized rs1; alu_b is the saved immediate for immediate operations,\notherwise finalized rs2. EX consumes these two operand flops directly,\nwith no late alu_src or is_auipc mux. Keep the existing independently\nprepared native multiplier inputs from raw rs1/rs2 and registered sign\nbits. Thus decode, register access and execution operand selection are\npartitioned across the new fetch/decode and existing execute boundaries;\nthis is one pipeline-topology change, not a collection of predictor or\narithmetic-unit redesigns.\n\nUse a one-entry elastic decoded-instruction slot. Fill only when imemReady\nis high and the slot is empty or the current head is genuinely consumed.\nAdvance the fetch PC exactly on fill. Permit simultaneous consume/refill\nfor one-instruction-per-cycle steady-state throughput and permit an empty\nslot to fill during an operand-stage hold. A full unconsumed slot retains\nall control, instruction, PC, immediate and prediction fields. A missing\nnew instruction-memory response must not erase the buffered instruction\nor prevent its decode/operand-stage consumption.\n\nDerive head consumption from its validity and actual ID/EX acceptance.\nDo not pop on a load-use bubble, division wait, older data-memory hold,\nreset or recovery. Compute load-use against the saved source fields and\npreserve the one-cycle load-to-use interlock and current-MEM load bypass.\nData-memory stalls retain the current request and finalized held operands;\nolder MEM/WB drains with the existing validity discipline. Do not add an\nextra result stage or a general register-dependency bubble.\n\nKeep immediate accepted-EX branch recovery and predictor training. On\nredirect, synchronously clear the decoded slot and ID/EX, discard the\nincoming wrong-path transaction and set the fetch PC to the resolved\ndestination even under imem backpressure or a full slot. Genuine EX holds\nstill suppress recovery at its source. The target instruction must enter\nthrough the new register on a later ready edge. Save the prediction used\non the original fetch acceptance and retain it unchanged through holds;\nsubsequent predictor training must not rewrite buffered metadata. Reset\nclears slot validity and restores PC=0. Keep the exact 64-entry predictor,\nits old-row behavior and all existing legality/alignment/range guards.\n\nPreserve all real RV32IM arithmetic and exact ALTOPS formulas, sticky\nonce-only division completion and older-stage draining. Division receives\nthe same raw architectural source values through the prepared operands.\nKeep decoder default legality, traps, memory bounds, once-only stores,\ncanonical CRCs and marker-bracketed timing. Retain nret=1 and the exact\ntop-level memory/RVFI port set with consecutive channel-0 retirement order.\nDo not modify any wrapper, benchmark, formal property/depth or evaluator.\n\nThe explicit cost is one additional recovery bubble per misprediction and\ninitial pipeline fill, not a new cycle for every arithmetic dependency.\nPrior replay counted about 62551 predictor misses in the 4197447-cycle\nbracket, approximately 1.5 percent first-order cycle cost. The elastic slot\ncan overlap some instruction stalls with backend holds. With a 1.5 percent\ncycle cost, median Fmax above approximately 82.40 MHz clears the 4.6 percent\nacceptance threshold. Predict an eight percent fitness improvement with\nlow confidence pending final vendor timing: remaining MEM-load extraction,\nEX-to-ID forwarding and predictor-update cones may still cap Fmax.\n\nValidate unchanged legality, arithmetic and retirement-cosim gates, plus\npipeline tests for paired saved controls/immediates, consume/refill,\nfull/empty holds, buffered decode with imemReady low, load-use, division,\nboth buses stalled, reset and wrong-path store/divider squashing. Run the\nunchanged nret=1 formal checks. Score canonical CRC-validated stalled\nCoreMark bracket cycles times median Gowin Fmax. Acceptance requires more\nthan 193.42 iter/sec; timing or area improvement alone is insufficient.\n'''\nh['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'}\nh['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Extend the accepted fetch bundle with decoded ctrl_t and immediate; add general alu_a/alu_b words to ID/EX alongside finalized raw operands and multiplier sign bits. Keep all metadata paired through stalls, reset and flush.'},\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Instantiate the unchanged decoder and immediate generator before a one-entry elastic registered fetch/decode transaction. Implement readiness-qualified fill, genuine head consumption, simultaneous replacement, full-slot hold and redirect-priority discard. Advance PC only on accepted fill or recovery, with no combinational decode bypass.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Consume saved controls and immediate instead of decoding live imem data. Read source addresses from the saved instruction, retain youngest-producer forwarding, and capture finalized raw operands plus PC/rs1-selected ALU A and immediate/rs2-selected ALU B on the existing ID/EX acceptance edge.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Drive ordinary ALU a/b directly from the prepared ID/EX words, removing late AUIPC and immediate operand muxes. Retain raw branch/store/RVFI sources, native multiplier formatting, division handshake, immediate accepted recovery and predictor training.'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Separate fetch-bus availability from consumption of the decoded head. Preserve load-use bubbles, division and data-memory holds; a missing new fetch cannot invalidate buffered work and a bubble cannot pop it. Keep existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB acceptance rules.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire registered decoded-head validity, imemReady and actual operand-stage consumption to the elastic frontend. Drive source checks and forwarding from the saved transaction; retain the exact nret=1 ports, predictor policy, arithmetic and retirement logic.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Preserve architectural assertions while adapting fetch-acceptance observations to the elastic slot. Cover controls/immediates paired with PC/instruction, back-to-back consume/refill, buffered progress during imem stalls, full/empty holds, all producer stages and ALU operand kinds, division, both bus stalls, recovery and reset. Require exact RVFI values/order, stable held requests and once-only memory effects.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote refined, schema-valid structural hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
