{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r11s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"Row-local predictor direction state without an indexed training read\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 64-entry tagged predictor already captures most of the\n  available CoreMark branch-cycle savings. Prior target banking, delayed\n  recovery and staged maintenance did not clear acceptance. Recent Gowin\n  paths repeatedly enter predictor direction-state maintenance from EX.\n  The present counter update reads a dynamically indexed counter and jump\n  bit, selects saturation or replacement, then writes through the indexed\n  port. Local state transitions can eliminate that read-selection cone\n  without adding training latency or changing predictor accuracy. There\n  is no LESSONS.md; the predictor history in log.jsonl was reviewed.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the predictor's direction-state maintenance architecture.\n  Keep all 64 logical entries, PC[7:2] indexing, twelve-bit tags, eighteen-\n  bit absolute target words, validity bitmap, asynchronous PC-only lookup,\n  two-bit bimodal policy and immediate accepted-EX recovery. Keep tag and\n  target payloads as the existing inferred memories. Implement each row's\n  two counter bits and jump-kind bit as constant-index, unreset flip-flops\n  in a generated row block: 192 state bits total. Do not replace the wide\n  tag/target memories with flops, bank the predictor, or pipeline training.\n\n  Each generated row computes its next counter directly from its own two\n  stored bits and its own jump bit. A row-local enable compares the update\n  index with that row's constant and uses the original accepted-update,\n  full-PC guard and legal-control-flow/target allocation qualification.\n  Reuse the original tag-hit decision to distinguish matching conditional\n  training from replacement; no dynamically addressed counter or jump\n  read participates in training. A matching conditional row saturates\n  according to the actual outcome. A tag replacement or jump-to-branch\n  kind change initializes 10 for taken and 01 for not taken; a jump wr\n…[2596 of 5668 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5734cacd1bec3d1f]…\natching, validity, exact policy and original update latency.\n      Use a balanced masked direction lookup tree with unchanged hit guards.\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing policy assertions. Add exhaustive transitions for all\n      four counter states and both outcomes, saturation endpoints, jump and\n      branch kind changes, every row, conflicting tags, back-to-back updates,\n      invalidation payload preservation, disabled updates and reset collisions.\n      Compare lookup results with an independent original-policy scoreboard.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all architectural assertions and add regressions combining\n      predictor training with fetch/load-use/dmem/division holds, consecutive\n      control flow, stale hits and changing JALR targets. Verify once-only EX\n      training, exact accepted predictions, immediate recovery and wrong-path\n      side-effect suppression, RVFI PC/link/operands/order and forward progress.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
