{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r12s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261005-003-r12s2\ntitle: \\\"Fetch-captured predictor training state with ordered row forwarding\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 64-entry BTB reads tags, jump kind and counters asynchronously for\n  both fetch and EX maintenance. Its update-side selection and saturation\n  repeatedly appear in long EX/predictor paths. CoreMark trace replay\n  indicates that index changes alone offer too little cycle improvement\n  to clear the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. Banking, delayed training\n  and fetch-target arithmetic have already regressed. LESSONS.md is absent;\n  the predictor history was reviewed. Move the maintenance read before the\n  existing execute launch boundary instead of adding another update stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retain the exact 64-row tagged BTB, PC[7:2] mapping, PC[19:8] tags,\n  target[19:2], two-bit saturation, jump policy and immediate EX-accepted\n  update visibility. Replace the update-side asynchronous tag, jump and\n  counter reads with one four-bit training snapshot captured from the\n  current fetch lookup on the same edge that ID/EX accepts its instruction:\n  lookup_hit, lookup_jump and lookup_counter[1:0]. Keep these registers\n  inside branch_predictor and add lookup_capture/lookup_clear controls\n  from core. Payload comes from the existing fetch read port; a miss or\n  out-of-range PC captures hit=0 and zero payload. No instruction stage,\n  extra lookup port, history table or target computation is introduced.\n\n  Align snapshot lifetime exactly with ID/EX. On reset or an ID flush,\n  clear it. Also clear when ID is advancing but fetch is unavailable,\n  because ID/EX receives a bubble. Capture only when \"'!stall_id,\n  !flush_id and !flush_if; otherwise retain it. In this core the concrete\n  clear control is flush_id || (!stall_id && flush_if), with reset handled\n  inside the predictor. Dmem and division holds retain the snapshot even\n  when imem is unavailable. Recovery flushes the younger snapshot while\n  the older accepted EX instruction still uses the pre-edge snapshot.\n  These controls must remain aligned with actual ID/EX validity, including\n  load-use bubbles and recovery priority over wrong-path interlocks.\n\n  Correct the old-row collision with a small last-committed-row register.\n  Record every actual table write: present, six-bit index, resulting\n  validity, twelve-bit tag, two-bit counter and jump kind. An allocation\n  records the complete newly written logical row. A matching invalidation\n  records validity=0; its other payload cannot authorize a hit. A no-op\n  or unrelated nonallocating tag miss does not replace this register.\n  Retain the last actual command through bubbles and all pipeline holds;\n  clearing its presence every cycle would break held-instruction training.\n  Reset clears presence and the existing table-valid bitmap.\n\n  EX maintenance starts from the saved lookup hit/kind/counter. If the\n  last committed index equals the current update index, instead use that\n  registered row, recomputing hit from its validity and full stored tag\n  against update_pc[19:8]. Forward invalidation as a miss, not as its old\n  counter. Since IF/ID is combinational and ID/EX is the only unexecuted\n  instruction register, the only write that can occur after a snapshot\n  was read and before its EX use is the preceding instruction'\"'s update\n  on the capture edge. During an EX hold no later instruction trains.\n  Thus this one ordered forwarding register supplies the exact current\n  logical row without rereading any update-addressed table payload.\n\n  Apply the original policy to that effective row: conditional replacement\n  and jump-to-branch changes start weak according to actual outcome;\n  matching conditional counters saturate; jumps overwrite the actual\n  target and unconditional kind. Preserve matching-only ordinary,\n  illegal, trapping and unrepresentable-target invalidation. Invalid PC\n  updates remain inert. Keep all full original PC/target range and\n  alignment guards before truncation, unreset table payload and reset-only\n  table validity. A not-taken branch trains its direct destination, never\n  PC+4. Table writes still occur on the original accepted EX edge, and\n  fetch acceptance on that edge still sees the old row. Do not forward\n  the saved command into fetch or alter the prediction that ID captured.\n\n  The intended gain is less update-side memory selection and routing:\n  maintenance now launches from small snapshot/command flops, while the\n  original fetch port serves both prediction and the next snapshot.\n  Preserve existing full-width architectural resolution, immediate\n  direction/target mismatch recovery, trap/link/RVFI behavior, synchronous\n  wrong-path killing, every memory/division interlock and once-only EX\n  training. Preserve nret=1 and top-level IO, decoder default illegality,\n  strict retirement order, progress, true/ALTOPS arithmetic, memory bounds,\n  canonical CRCs, completion text, markers and all correctness gates.\n\n  Estimate seven percent fitness improvement with low confidence. This\n  snapshot-and-ordered-forwarding architecture is distinct from delayed\n  bank-local maintenance: there is no added training latency or bank.\n  Extra snapshot muxing, collision comparison and residual ALU/ID paths\n  may erase its gain. Do not claim an acceptance win without unchanged\n  canonical stalled CoreMark validation and median Gowin timing. At\n  4,197,447 bracket cycles, acceptance requires more than approximately\n  85.286 MHz and fitness above 203.1855 iterations/sec.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the fetch-captured hit/jump/counter snapshot and capture/clear\n      controls. Replace update-indexed tag/counter/jump reads with the\n      saved probe plus registered last-actual-write forwarding. Retain\n      complete original target/direction policy, guards, storage mapping,\n      same-edge writes and old-row fetch behavior. Retain the last command\n      through disabled updates and holds; reset cancels its presence.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect snapshot capture to \"'!stall_id && !flush_id && !flush_if and\n      clear to flush_id || (!stall_id && flush_if), matching ID/EX lifetime.\n      Preserve existing predictor lookup/update signals, accepted EX\n      training, immediate recovery, hazards and all external ports.\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt standalone stimulus to the fetch-capture/EX-update protocol\n      and compare every resulting logical row against an independent\n      model of the original policy. Cover all rows, back-to-back same-PC\n      saturation, different tags sharing an index, jump/branch changes,\n      allocation and invalidation on the snapshot edge, disabled updates,\n      old-row lookup collisions, reset and full-address guards. Explicitly\n      retain a probe through several hold/bubble edges and verify that\n      the last actual command still repairs its stale snapshot.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Keep every existing architectural and predictor-policy assertion.\n      Add snapshot lifetime and command-collision checks for simultaneous\n      lookup/training, matching stale invalidation, conflicting tags,\n      load-use bubbles, imem stalls, prolonged dmem/division holds,\n      recovery flushes and reset. Require identical prediction decisions\n      to the original model, once-only training and memory effects, exact\n      RVFI PCs/links/operands/order, traps and bounded forward progress.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r12s2.yaml'\nh=yaml.safe_load(open(p));jsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261005-003-r12s2' and h['category']=='predictor'\nassert -50 <= h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <=50\nprint('Schema-valid hypothesis written:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
