{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_25", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Fold higher PC bits into the unchanged 64-entry target predictor index\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 194.25 iterations/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4,197,447\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its 64-entry tagged predictor indexes\n  solely by PC[7:2], so control transfers separated by multiples of 256\n  bytes evict each other's targets and direction counters. Recent predictor\n  changes to banking, training pipelines and counter storage lost Fmax;\n  separating untagged direction history saved only 259 cycles. LESSONS.md\n  is absent. The earlier hashed predictor also shrank capacity to sixteen\n  entries and changed metadata, so it did not isolate index distribution\n  at the champion's original capacity and storage topology.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the address mapping of the existing 64-entry tagged\n  target-and-direction predictor. Use the same six-bit folded index for\n  fetch and accepted EX updates: PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Keep the twelve-bit\n  tag PC[19:8], eighteen-bit target word, two-bit saturating counter, jump\n  bit, 64-bit validity vector, inferred native arrays and original training\n  edge. The stored tag includes all higher bits used by the hash; together\n  the index and tag still uniquely identify every aligned lower-1-MiB PC.\n  Do not reduce capacity, bank storage, add ports or bypasses, pipeline\n  maintenance, change direction policy, or add history state.\n\n  The intended gain is fewer destructive conflicts between hot branches\n  and jumps, preserving their trained counters and last targets. The only\n  new lookup/update arithmetic is six parallel two-input XORs. A miss\n  remains not-taken with zero target; a guarded tag hit retains the exact\n  original jump/direction decision and target output. Preserve weak\n  outcome-based initialization on replacement, saturating updates on\n  matching conditional rows, jump-to-branch reinitialization and matching-\n  only stale invalidation. All range/alignment checks use the full original\n  PC and target befo\n…[1204 of 4276 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1d2a2e74b76774f9]…\nctor.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace lookup_index and update_index PC[7:2] slices with the same\n      six-bit PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] hash. Keep all 64 rows, the original\n      PC[19:8] tags, payload arrays, validity, update policy, full-address\n      guards and combinational/synchronous boundaries unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all behavioral assertions; update only collision addresses\n      that intentionally depended on the original linear index. Add cases\n      where PCs 256 bytes apart now coexist, distinct full PCs with equal\n      folded indices replace each other, upper-tag differences remain\n      distinguishable, and same-tag/different-low-PC entries remain distinct.\n      Exercise all rows, saturation, kind changes, stale invalidation,\n      disabled/illegal/out-of-range updates, reset and old-row lookup on\n      simultaneous update. Compare against an independent hash-indexed\n      model and retain the existing architectural/formal/cosim gates.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
