{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Single-bit last-outcome direction removes predictor read-modify-write\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 194.25 iterations/sec at 81.537 MHz and 4,197,447\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Reported vendor paths repeatedly cross\n  EX, predictor maintenance and ID operand launch. The predictor reads its\n  old counter through an update-addressed memory port, saturates it using\n  the late branch outcome, then writes it back. Recent banking, pipelined\n  training, fetch snapshots and flop-counter experiments lost Fmax.\n  LESSONS.md is absent. Independent control-flow replay found that index\n  hashing saves fewer than 8,000 mispredictions, whereas changing to a\n  single last-outcome bit adds approximately 22,690. That cost is about\n  0.54 percent of current cycles before stall interaction, making removal\n  of the counter feedback path a plausible net fitness trade.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace each two-bit saturating direction counter with one last-outcome\n  bit in the existing 64-entry tagged target predictor. This is one\n  direction-policy and maintenance simplification. Keep PC[7:2] indexing,\n  PC[19:8] tags, eighteen-bit target words, jump bits, validity, original\n  native payload arrays, asynchronous PC-only fetch lookup and immediate\n  EX-accepted training. Do not hash, resize or bank the table, add global\n  history, pipeline training, change recovery latency or alter any other\n  instruction datapath.\n\n  On a guarded tag hit, predict taken for a jump or a conditional row whose\n  direction bit is one. On every original legal representable branch/jump\n  allocation, write the direction bit directly from update_taken, forcing\n  one for jumps. A matching branch no longer reads its previous direction\n  state during training, and replacement or jump-to-branch transitions use\n  exactly the accepted outcome. Remove next_counter, saturation arithmetic\n  and the update-addressed counter read. Keep the sole direction read at\n  lookup_index and one synchronous write port, s\n…[2624 of 5696 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:22c8f6c998255d3b]…\npredictor.py\n    description: |\n      Update predictor-policy expectations for the intentional last-outcome\n      behavior: every accepted conditional outcome immediately controls\n      its next prediction, including alternating outcomes and both loop\n      boundaries. Retain range/alignment, tag alias/replacement, jump target\n      changes, kind transitions, stale invalidation, disabled writes, reset\n      and old-row simultaneous lookup/update checks. Add randomized\n      comparison with an independent one-bit tagged predictor model.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve architectural assertions and add repeated loop/exit and\n      alternating branch regressions under both memory stalls. Check\n      captured predictions survive holds, recovery trains once, wrong-path\n      stores/register writes never commit, and exact RVFI PC continuity,\n      links, order and bounded progress hold. Keep all existing formal,\n      legality, arithmetic, cosim and canonical CoreMark gates intact.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
