{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Two-way tagged target buffer to retain conflicting hot branches\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 64-entry direct-mapped predictor reduced canonical stalled\n  CoreMark cycles from 4491817 to 4197447 and achieved fitness 117.38 at\n  49.269 MHz. Every control-transfer PC separated by a multiple of 256 bytes\n  competes for the same row, replacing both its target and learned direction.\n  CoreMark repeatedly visits several functions and loops, so destructive\n  target-buffer conflicts can recreate cold predictions throughout the run.\n  There is no LESSONS.md. The earlier instruction-decoded predictor ran on\n  the old combinational-divider core; it does not establish a regression\n  for improving predictor accuracy on the current champion. The champion's\n  reported limiting path remains through register reads, EX and the ALU.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the direct-mapped target buffer with a 64-set, two-way tagged\n  target buffer, giving 128 compact entries. This hypothesis targets target\n  residency and conflict misses. Retain the existing per-entry two-bit\n  bimodal direction counter and unconditional JAL/JALR target prediction;\n  do not add instruction-memory decoding, global history, a return stack,\n  a fetch stage, or changes to the execution pipeline and arithmetic.\n\n  Index both ways by PC[7:2], retain tag PC[19:8], and store target[19:2],\n  two-bit counter and jump kind in each row. Lookup reads the two 64-row\n  banks in parallel, compares both tags independently, and selects the\n  matching way's target and direction. There must be at most one valid\n  matching entry per set. A miss predicts not taken. Preserve the lower\n  1-MiB and word-alignment guards on PCs and targets, including zero upper\n  target bits, so compact tags cannot alias out-of-range fetch addresses.\n\n  For an EX-accepted, legal, representable control transfer, update the\n  matching way in place. On a miss, choose an invalid way before evicting\n  a valid entry; if both ways are valid, use one re\n…[3193 of 6265 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9c9a2b7b70fee5db]…\nree-tag eviction checks,\n      invalid-way preference, independent counters and jump kinds per way,\n      hit updates that do not evict the other way, nonmatching ordinary\n      updates, kind changes, and simultaneous lookup/update in each way.\n      Ensure reset wins over updates and clears both valid maps.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add a directed program alternating two hot control transfers whose\n      PCs differ by 256 bytes, including differing direction histories and\n      recurrent JALR targets, to exercise useful same-set residency. Add a\n      third conflicting tag to exercise eviction and correct EX recovery.\n      Use existing edge-accurate imem/dmem stalls and load-use/divider holds\n      around conflict updates and recovery. Assert exact retirement PCs,\n      resolved RVFI next PCs and order, register results, accepted-store\n      counts, no wrong-path effects and bounded progress. Retain all prior\n      tests and run the unchanged formal, cosim and CoreMark gates.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
