d ID/EX. Advance the PC to that prediction only when the existing\nfetch/decode handshake accepts the instruction. Prediction must not advance\nthe PC during imem backpressure, load-use interlocks, or dmem backpressure.\nIn EX retain the existing forwarded operands, branch comparison, target\ncalculation, jump link value, and misalignment/trap behavior. Compute the\narchitectural next PC using those existing rules, including PC+4 on a\nsuppressed misaligned control transfer. Redirect and flush only when a\nvalid advancing EX instruction's resolved next PC differs from its saved\nprediction, rather than on every taken control transfer. Keep the resolved\nnext PC as the source of RVFI pc_wdata. Correct predictions admit the\nfetche\n…[14520 of 17592 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ea550f9e41339fcd]…\nery arithmetic and\ndecoder rule, retirement order, memory handshake and hard invariant.\nChange only the predictor implementation and its allowed unit tests.\n\nAn isolated four-bank prototype passed all existing predictor policy\ntests, selftest retirement cosim with and without bus stalls, and\ncanonical stalled CoreMark CRC/completion/bounds/bracketing validation.\nCoreMark remained exactly 4197447 bracket cycles for ten iterations.\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 51.985, 55.420 and 55.420 MHz,\nwith 3552 logic cells, 925 registers, 60 LUT-RAM units, six BSRAMs and\ntwo DSPs. Median fitness is approximately 132.033 iter/sec, a 4.41\npercent improvement over 126.46. This prototype is still below the\nrequired 132.277 acceptance threshold; do not claim a champion win.\nThe expected integer gain is 4 percent with medium confidence.\nFull formal validation and a fresh vendor median remain required for\nimplementation. Remaining recovery/arithmetic timing and extra\ndistributed-memory routing are the material risks.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
