{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r5s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-003-r5s2',\n'title':'Parallel-match flop BTB with balanced target selection',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 170.74 iter/sec at median Fmax 71.668 MHz and\n4197447 canonical stalled CoreMark bracket cycles. Its predictor uses\nvariable-index asynchronous tag, target, counter and jump arrays. These\ncan infer different FPGA memory structures and require selected-tag\ncomparison after a table read. Earlier capacity reduction sacrificed\naccuracy, while target banking alone regressed; neither tested constant-row\nflops with parallel hit detection. No LESSONS.md exists.\n\nA temporary baseline simulator, built without editing the RTL or evaluation\nharness, reproduced exactly 4197447 bracket cycles, completed in bounds and\npassed the canonical CRC validator. Sequential predictor replay of its\n2922016 timed retirements counted 62551 misses. A small four-bit local-history\npredictor with 512 pattern counters removed only 1872 of those misses;\na 16-entry return stack removed 1749. These are opportunity estimates,\nnot cycle-accurate pipeline predictions. Their small savings argue for\npreserving predictor accuracy while improving its physical implementation.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Reimplement the existing 64-entry direct-mapped target/direction predictor\nas 64 generated constant-index flop rows and a balanced masked-OR read tree.\nThis is one predictor timing and memory-mapping experiment: keep the exact\ncapacity, PC mapping, tag/target widths, bimodal policy and prediction latency.\nChange only branch_predictor.sv and its allowed policy tests. Add no history\npredictor, return stack, stage, training delay or arithmetic redesign.\n\nEach generated row owns one valid bit, a 12-bit PC tag, an 18-bit aligned\ntarget word, a two-bit saturating counter and one jump bit. Use only scalar\nconstant-row registers; no dynamically indexed payload read or write array\nmay remain. The local lookup-hit signal is lookup-in-range AND valid AND\nPC[7:2] equals that row's six-bit constant AND its tag equals PC[19:8].\nCompare each row's tag in parallel with index qualification, rather than\nselecting a tag through a 64-way read mux before comparing it. Each leaf\nemits a 19-bit vector: the row's hit-qualified taken bit followed by its\n18 target bits masked by hit. OR pairs through an explicit balanced six-level\nbinary tree. Root bit 18 drives predicted_taken and the lower 18 bits\nreconstruct predicted_target as {12'b0, target_word, 2'b0}. Mask targets by\nhit, including not-taken hits, so all current predictor output semantics\nremain exact. Require both outputs zero on misses or invalid lookups.\n\nFor updates, separately decode update_pc[7:2] against each constant row and\ngate with update_en, update-in-range and \"'!reset. Only that row may change.\nCompute its matching-tag and counter saturation locally from its own flops.\nAllocate only legal branch/jump updates with representable aligned targets.\nA replacement or jump-to-branch kind change starts weak according to actual\noutcome; matching conditional rows saturate exactly as today. Jumps overwrite\nactual targets and always predict taken. Ordinary, illegal, trapping or\nunrepresentable updates invalidate only the matching PC'\"'s row, preserving\nan unrelated tag. Reset only valid bits, inhibits all payload writes and\nleaves tag/target/counter/jump registers unreset. Lookup before an update\nedge observes old state; all payload and validity changes become visible\natomically after that edge. Keep the public predictor port set identical.\n\nKeep current PC-only lookup and full-width range/alignment checks. Do not\nput instruction-memory data on the prediction path. Preserve fetch metadata,\nnormal acceptance under imem/load-use/dmem/division stalls, synchronous\nwrong-path killing, immediate EX mismatch recovery and recovery priority.\nEX remains responsible for architectural direction, targets and traps;\nprediction remains advisory. Preserve all current RTL outside the predictor,\nincluding the true operand-register boundary, multiplier/divider behavior,\nnret=1 IO/RVFI, decoder defaults, monotonic retirement, memory handshakes,\ncanonical CRCs, address bounds and benchmark timing markers.\n\nThe initial expectation is a six-percent fitness gain with low confidence,\nentirely from Fmax at unchanged benchmark cycles. The explicit rows should\nprevent predictor payload RAM inference and shorten the serial lookup mux\nplus tag-compare path. They use roughly 2240 predictor state flops in place\nof inferred memory; added logic and routing may outweigh the gain, and the\nchampion's current worst path is in the ALU rather than the predictor.\nRequire unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles and vendor median Fmax above\napproximately 74.965 MHz to clear the 4.6-percent acceptance margin.\nMeasure actual Gowin timing and resources; do not assume a faster logical\nlookup guarantees better whole-core timing. Keep every correctness gate\nand don't-touch file intact.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/branch_predictor.sv','description':'''Replace variable-index predictor payload arrays with 64 generated scalar\nflop rows and constant-index write decoding. Form hit-qualified direction\nand target leaves with parallel tag matching, then reduce them through a\nbalanced 19-bit OR tree. Preserve every interface, reset, output, allocation,\ntraining, replacement, stale invalidation and old-row lookup rule.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_branch_predictor.py','description':'''Keep all current predictor policy assertions. Extend coverage across all\n64 row indices, minimum/maximum tags and targets, same-index tag conflicts,\nnot-taken hits that still report their target, and unrelated-row isolation.\nCheck simultaneous lookup/update/replacement, disabled updates, reset\nwinning over payload writes, jump/branch kind changes and range guards.\nRequire architectural pipeline tests and unchanged stalled retirement/CoreMark\ncosim plus the existing formal and median Gowin evaluation gates.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=80,default_style=None))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised complete hypothesis validates against the schema')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
