{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, yaml, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r6s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-003-r6s2\ntitle: \\\"Shallow banked synchronous lookahead target predictor\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 184.91 iterations/second at 77.615 MHz with 4197447\n  canonical stalled CoreMark bracket cycles. Its reported worst path crosses\n  EX, predictor storage and ID. The existing 64-entry predictor already has\n  good accuracy; the promising opportunity is the timing of target lookup,\n  prediction capture and recovery, rather than more predictor capacity.\n\n  LESSONS.md is absent. Prior history estimates only 62551 remaining misses;\n  associativity, small history tables and a return stack offer insufficient\n  cycle savings to clear the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. Capacity\n  reduction, asynchronous target banking and MEM-based training failed.\n  On this champion, isolated training registration measures 74.527 MHz.\n  Registering a lookahead prediction outside the asynchronous table measures\n  78.486 MHz and 4198782 bracket cycles, with all canonical CRCs and eight\n  stalled/zero-wait retirement comparisons passing. An explicit synchronous\n  BSRAM target read measures 77.497 MHz. None meets acceptance. The external\n  lookahead version exposes a target-memory feedback path; use shallow\n  distributed banks with explicit synchronous reads to shorten that loop.\nhypothesis: |\n  Implement one synchronous next-PC lookahead target predictor, retaining\n  exactly the existing 64 direct-mapped entries and prediction policy.\n  Partition target storage into four 16-word, 18-bit banks indexed by\n  PC[7:6], with PC[5:2] selecting a row. Use constant bank instances and\n  small unreset arrays, with an enabled synchronous read register per bank\n  and a registered bank selector. Read all four banks at the selected row\n  on a lookup advance, then select the registered target with a balanced\n  four-input mux. This is approximately 1152 target-memory bits plus 72\n  read-register bits, not a broad flop replacement or additional capacity.\n  Target storage should infer shallow distributed LUT memory rather than\n  an asynchronous 64-word BSRAM plus fabric output qualification. Keep the\n  current tag, valid, jump and counter storage organization unless a small\n  indexing adjustment is necessary to address the same logical row.\n\n  Predictor outputs are registered and describe the current fetch PC;\n  the lookup address describes the normal selected next PC. In IF compute\n  next_pc from the current registered prediction, choosing its target when\n  taken and PC+4 otherwise. On an ordinary accepted fetch edge, advance PC\n  to next_pc and register the bank reads, bank selector and direction for\n  that same address. The following cycle then already has both its PC and\n  prediction. Lookup must never depend on its own unregistered direction\n  or target output. Preserve one accepted fetch per cycle with one logical\n  lookup port; do not add an instruction pipeline stage or a fetch queue.\n\n  Add lookup_advance and lookup_clear controls. lookup_advance is\n  \"'!reset && !redirect && !stall_if. Hold every registered prediction field\n  together with PC under imem backpressure, load-use interlocks, dmem holds\n  and division waits. Register direction from the existing PC range and\n  alignment guards, valid bit, tag match and jump-or-counter-MSB policy.\n  Keep tag-hit, valid, reset and miss-to-zero multiplexers outside the raw\n  bank read-register inputs. Read payload and bank selector need not reset:\n  reset clears table validity and registered predicted_taken, which makes\n  unknown payload harmless. Reconstruct targets with twelve zero upper bits\n  and two zero alignment bits. When direction is false, raw target payload\n  must have no effect on PC, validity, architectural state or RVFI.\n\n  Accepted EX recovery still immediately writes redirect_target to PC and\n  overrides every front-end stall. On that edge lookup_clear clears direction,\n  lookup_advance is false and target payload may hold. Conservatively predict\n  sequentially for the first fetch after reset/recovery, then prime the next\n  lookahead on normal acceptance. Never place a lookup of combinational\n  redirect_target after the EX target adder. This may add a few cold misses\n  but cannot delay architectural recovery. Capture exact prediction metadata\n  with each instruction and retain it through every ID/EX hold.\n\n  Training stays at the existing once-per-valid-EX-acceptance boundary.\n  Only the addressed target bank writes; read/update at the same edge uses\n  the old row in RTL. Preserve saturation, weak replacement initialization,\n  unconditional jump behavior, changing indirect targets, jump-to-branch\n  reinitialization, target range/alignment guards and matching-only stale\n  invalidation. Not-taken branches train their direct destination, never\n  PC+4. Predictor state remains advisory and EX independently resolves all\n  architectural outcomes and full-width targets, including stale hits.\n\n  Factor predictor control-flow eligibility independently of branch outcome\n  to prevent a resolved comparator from feeding trap logic before predictor\n  resolution. branch_eligible is decoded legal branch AND aligned destination;\n  jump_eligible is decoded legal jump AND aligned destination. actual_taken is\n  jump_eligible OR (branch_eligible AND branch_cond). A taken prediction'\"'s\n  target inequality can be checked without actual_taken gating, because\n  direction mismatch already recovers when actual_taken is false. Predictor\n  allocation may reject an unaligned branch destination regardless of outcome,\n  as its existing target guard already does. Preserve ctrl_with_trap and all\n  architectural trap/next-PC behavior. Remove branch-trap outcome from unrelated\n  EX producer gating while retaining decoded reg_write, decoded legality and\n  jump-alignment suppression: decoded branches never write rd. Preserve\n  forwarding priority, arithmetic and all instruction-stage boundaries.\n\n  Estimate an 8 percent fitness gain with low confidence. This is a new\n  banked synchronous implementation; the simpler measured prototypes above\n  are below acceptance and do not establish a win. At 4198782 bracket cycles\n  the median Fmax must exceed about 81.22 MHz to clear acceptance. The main\n  risks are distributed-memory inference, bank-mux routing, cold/recovery\n  misses and the remaining ALU-to-ID forwarding path. Verify actual memory\n  mapping in Gowin reports; avoid wholesale constant-row flop arrays, which\n  previously increased area without sufficient timing benefit. Score only\n  actual median Gowin Fmax times canonical marker-bracketed iterations/cycle\n  with the existing random bus stalls. Preserve nret=1 and all top-level IO,\n  exact RVFI/order/PC behavior, decoder defaults, traps, memory bounds,\n  true multiplier/divider and ALTOPS semantics, canonical CRCs and every\n  unchanged correctness and fitness gate. Modify only allowed RTL/tests.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add enabled synchronous next-PC lookahead outputs. Partition only\n      target payload into four constant 16x18 distributed-memory banks with\n      unreset read registers, registered bank selection and a balanced output\n      mux. Keep direction qualification separate from raw reads. Preserve all\n      64 logical entries, EX training semantics, guards, counters, jump kinds,\n      reset validity and matching-only invalidation. Hold read state on stalls\n      and clear registered direction on reset/recovery.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the registered current-PC prediction and expose normal next_pc as\n      the lookup address. Advance lookup and PC on the same accepted edge;\n      hold both on every existing stall. Expose lookup advance/clear controls\n      and clear direction on accepted recovery without looking up its target.\n      Preserve exact metadata, immediate PC recovery and valid/NOP masking.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire next-PC lookup and its advance/clear handshake between IF and the\n      predictor, replacing current-PC lookup. Keep EX training, memory buses,\n      hazard controls, arithmetic and the nret=1 top-level contract intact.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Factor legal aligned predictor eligibility before branch outcome and\n      remove actual_taken from the target-mismatch term. Decouple branch-trap\n      outcome from unrelated EX producers while retaining illegal and jump\n      alignment suppression. Preserve architectural trap controls, exact\n      results, immediate accepted recovery and all EX/MEM payload semantics.\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt lookup driving to synchronous advance/clear and retain all\n      meaningful policy, saturation, replacement, kind-change, invalidation,\n      range/alignment and reset coverage. Exercise every bank and row, cross-\n      bank transitions, output holds, same-row lookup/write old-data capture,\n      simultaneous different-bank training, clear priority and unknown invalid\n      payload isolation. Check targets when direction is valid.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update the internal prediction timing model for registered current-PC\n      metadata while retaining all architectural retirement/source/result/order,\n      trap and wrong-path-effect assertions. Retain loops, exits, changing\n      JALRs, conflicts, stale code and misalignment; add reset/recovery priming,\n      bank transitions, lookup/update collisions and holds under every stall.\n      Adapt directed wrong-path stimuli to the extra lookup latency so load-use\n      and store-poison recovery remain exercised. Require unchanged formal,\n      cosim, arithmetic/decoder tests and canonical CoreMark/vendor validation.\n''')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261005-003-r6s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Wrote and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
