{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261005-003-r3s2','hyp-20261005-003-r4s2']:\n  print(d['id']); print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-003-r3s2\nReplace the predictor with one compact 16-entry direct-mapped tagged\ntarget/direction table and carry its exact taken target as an 18-bit\nword address. This is one predictor-footprint optimization: trade a\nmodest increase in target conflicts for smaller tables, simpler lookup\nselection and narrower metadata routing. Keep PC-only asynchronous\nlookup, two-bit bimodal counters, unconditional JAL/JALR entries and the\nchampion's EX recovery protocol. Do not change pipeline depth, arithmetic,\nretirement width, memory handshakes or the recovery penalty.\n\nUse the same four-bit hash for lookup and update:\nindex = PC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6]. Store tag PC[19:6] (14 bits), target[19:2]\n(18 bits), the two-bit counter and jump bit in 16-row arrays. Keep a\nseparate 16-bit valid bitmap, reset only that bitmap, and leave payload\narrays unreset for memory inference. A tag match plus equal hash index\nuniquely identifies all aligned PC bits within the lower 1 MiB: the\nstored tag includes the high hash operand, so equality also implies\nequality of PC[5:2]. Retain the current PC[31:20]==0, PC[1:0]==0 and\ntarget range/alignment guards. Never truncate a PC or target before its\nguard. No extra pipeline stage, additional read/write port or lookup\ndependence on instruction-memory data is introduced.\n\nPreserve every existing update rule with the new dimensions and tag:\nallocate only EX-accepted legal representable branches/jumps, initialize\nreplacement conditional counters weakly according to outcome, saturate\nmatching conditional counters, and overwrite actual JAL/JALR targets.\nA jump-to-conditional kind change starts weak. Ordinary, illegal,\ntrapping and unrepresentable updates invalidate only their matching tag;\nunrelated tags survive. Lookup accepted on an update edge uses pre-edge\nstate. Holds and divider waits must not repeat training. The hash changes\ncollision policy, never architectural instruction or trap semantics.\n\nNarrow predicted_target in both if_id_t and id_ex_t from 32 to 18 bits.\nIn IF emit predicted_target[\n…[4292 of 7364 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:26298ad105bc2bc8]…\nry arithmetic and\ndecoder rule, retirement order, memory handshake and hard invariant.\nChange only the predictor implementation and its allowed unit tests.\n\nAn isolated four-bank prototype passed all existing predictor policy\ntests, selftest retirement cosim with and without bus stalls, and\ncanonical stalled CoreMark CRC/completion/bounds/bracketing validation.\nCoreMark remained exactly 4197447 bracket cycles for ten iterations.\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 51.985, 55.420 and 55.420 MHz,\nwith 3552 logic cells, 925 registers, 60 LUT-RAM units, six BSRAMs and\ntwo DSPs. Median fitness is approximately 132.033 iter/sec, a 4.41\npercent improvement over 126.46. This prototype is still below the\nrequired 132.277 acceptance threshold; do not claim a champion win.\nThe expected integer gain is 4 percent with medium confidence.\nFull formal validation and a fresh vendor median remain required for\nimplementation. Remaining recovery/arithmetic timing and extra\ndistributed-memory routing are the material risks.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
